/* Pharmacology 8–9: Blood & GIT */
Z("pharm8",0,"ph",["Iron is given orally (ferrous sulfate, fumarate, gluconate) for most iron deficiency anaemia; parenteral iron (iron sucrose, ferric carboxymaltose, iron dextran) is used when oral iron fails, is not tolerated or rapid repletion is needed.","Oral iron is the slow, cheap road; parenteral iron is the express highway used when the slow road is blocked (malabsorption) or time is short (late pregnancy).",["Oral: ferrous salts 100–200 mg elemental iron/day (60 mg + folic acid prophylaxis in pregnancy); best on empty stomach, vitamin C ↑ absorption; tea, antacids, tetracyclines, calcium ↓.","Oral ADR: epigastric pain, nausea, constipation/diarrhoea, black stools, metallic taste, tooth staining (liquids).","Parenteral indications: intolerance/non-compliance, malabsorption (IBD, celiac, post-gastrectomy), ongoing blood loss, severe anaemia late in pregnancy, CKD with ESA, chemotherapy.","Parenteral ADR: anaphylaxis (iron dextran — test dose), hypotension, arthralgia, fever, phlebitis, staining (IM — Z-track); iron sucrose and ferric carboxymaltose safer."],"Response: reticulocytosis 5–10 days, Hb ↑ 1 g/dL per 1–2 weeks; continue 3–6 months to refill stores."],
"Iron is essential for haemoglobin, myoglobin and enzymes; iron deficiency anaemia (microcytic hypochromic) is the commonest nutritional deficiency, especially in women, children and pregnancy in India. Oral iron preparations: ferrous sulphate (hydrated 20% elemental iron; cheapest), ferrous fumarate (33%), ferrous gluconate (12%), ferrous succinate, iron polysaccharide complex, carbonyl iron, ferric ammonium citrate, ferrous bisglycinate. Ferrous (Fe²⁺) salts are absorbed better than ferric. Absorption occurs in duodenum and upper jejunum via DMT1, regulated by hepcidin; enhanced by acid, ascorbic acid, meat, deficiency states; reduced by phosphates, phytates, tannins (tea), antacids, PPIs, calcium, tetracyclines, food. Dose: 100–200 mg elemental iron daily in divided doses (or 60–120 mg alternate-day dosing — improved absorption by avoiding hepcidin rise); prophylaxis in pregnancy (Anaemia Mukt Bharat) 60 mg elemental iron + 500 µg folic acid daily; children syrup 1–2 mg/kg. Response: subjective improvement within days, reticulocytosis peaks at 7–10 days, Hb rises about 1 g/dL every 1–2 weeks (0.1–0.2 g/dL/day); continue 3–6 months after Hb normalises to replenish stores (ferritin). Uses: treatment and prophylaxis of iron deficiency anaemia (pregnancy, infancy, adolescence, menorrhagia, hookworm infestation, chronic blood loss). Adverse effects of oral iron: epigastric discomfort, nausea, vomiting, heartburn, constipation (or diarrhoea), metallic taste, black stools, staining of teeth (liquid — use straw); taking with food reduces side effects at the cost of absorption. Parenteral iron: iron dextran (IM/IV; risk of anaphylaxis — test dose; total dose infusion possible), iron sorbitol citric acid (IM only), iron sucrose (IV, 100–200 mg per dose; safe), ferric carboxymaltose (IV, up to 1000 mg in single infusion over 15 min; preferred in pregnancy after first trimester), ferric derisomaltose, ferumoxytol. Total iron requirement (mg) = 4.4 × body weight (kg) × Hb deficit (g/dL) (or Ganzoni formula) + 500–1000 mg for stores. Indications: intolerance to or non-compliance with oral iron; malabsorption (IBD, celiac disease, gastrectomy, bariatric surgery); continuing blood loss exceeding oral replacement; severe anaemia in late pregnancy (second/third trimester) or before surgery; CKD patients on erythropoietin and dialysis; chemotherapy-induced anaemia; heart failure with iron deficiency. Parenteral iron does not raise Hb faster than optimal oral iron but ensures repletion. Adverse effects: local pain, brown discolouration of skin, sterile abscess (IM — use Z-track technique), phlebitis, headache, fever, arthralgia, lymphadenopathy, urticaria, hypotension, hypophosphataemia (ferric carboxymaltose), anaphylactoid reactions (mostly with high-molecular-weight iron dextran); iron overload with repeated doses.",
[["Oral vs parenteral iron",[["Feature","Oral","Parenteral"],["Preparations","Ferrous sulphate, fumarate, gluconate","Iron sucrose, ferric carboxymaltose, iron dextran"],["Cost","Cheap","Expensive"],["Speed of Hb rise","Similar","Similar (stores filled faster)"],["ADR","GI upset, constipation","Anaphylaxis, phlebitis, staining, arthralgia"],["Use","First choice","Intolerance, malabsorption, late pregnancy, CKD"]]],
["Indications for parenteral iron",["Intolerance/non-compliance to oral iron","Malabsorption","Ongoing blood loss","Severe anaemia in late pregnancy","CKD on ESA/dialysis","Before surgery"]],
["Factors affecting absorption",[["Increase","Decrease"],["Vitamin C, acid, meat","Tea, phytates, phosphates"],["Ferrous form","Antacids, PPIs, calcium"],["Iron deficiency","Tetracyclines, food"]]]],
{c:"Oral ferrous salts remain first-line for iron deficiency; parenteral iron is reserved for specific indications, with newer formulations offering safer, rapid repletion."});

Z("pharm8",1,"ph",["Acute iron poisoning, mostly in children who swallow iron tablets, causes vomiting, bloody diarrhoea, shock and liver failure; desferrioxamine is the specific chelating antidote.","Children mistake iron tablets for sweets; free iron corrodes the gut and poisons cells.",["Toxic dose > 20 mg/kg elemental iron; > 60 mg/kg potentially lethal.","Stages: GI (0–6 h: vomiting, haematemesis, diarrhoea), latent (6–24 h), shock & metabolic acidosis (12–48 h), hepatotoxicity (2–5 days), late gastric/pyloric stricture (2–8 weeks).","Investigations: serum iron (> 500 µg/dL severe), X-ray abdomen (radio-opaque tablets), ABG.","Treatment: supportive (fluids), whole bowel irrigation (charcoal ineffective), IV desferrioxamine 15 mg/kg/h (urine vin rosé colour), correct acidosis, avoid prolonged DFO > 24 h (ARDS)."],"Iron poisoning antidote: DesFerrioxamine (DFO) — 'Defeats Fe'."],
"Acute iron poisoning is a common accidental poisoning in young children (iron tablets resemble sweets) and occasionally in suicidal adults. Toxicity depends on the amount of elemental iron ingested: < 20 mg/kg — usually asymptomatic; 20–60 mg/kg — moderate toxicity; > 60 mg/kg — severe, potentially fatal. Pathophysiology: direct corrosive injury to gastrointestinal mucosa (haemorrhagic necrosis); absorbed iron exceeds transferrin binding capacity, and free iron catalyses free-radical formation and lipid peroxidation, damages mitochondria (lactic acidosis), causes vasodilatation and capillary leak (hypotension), coagulopathy and hepatic necrosis. Clinical stages: Stage I (0–6 h) — GI phase: vomiting, abdominal pain, haematemesis, diarrhoea, melaena, lethargy; Stage II (6–24 h) — latent/quiescent phase (apparent improvement; may be absent in severe poisoning); Stage III (12–48 h) — shock and metabolic acidosis: hypovolaemic and distributive shock, cardiomyopathy, coagulopathy, renal failure, coma, convulsions; Stage IV (2–5 days) — hepatotoxicity: hepatic necrosis, hypoglycaemia, jaundice; Stage V (2–8 weeks) — GI scarring: pyloric/gastric outlet obstruction, strictures. Investigations: serum iron at 4–6 hours (> 350 µg/dL moderate, > 500 µg/dL severe), TIBC (unreliable), ABG (acidosis), glucose, LFTs, coagulation, X-ray abdomen (radiopaque tablets). Treatment: (1) supportive — airway, IV fluids for hypovolaemia, correction of acidosis, blood transfusion if needed; (2) decontamination — activated charcoal does not bind iron; gastric lavage of limited value; whole bowel irrigation with polyethylene glycol if tablets seen on X-ray; endoscopic/surgical removal for bezoars; (3) chelation — desferrioxamine (deferoxamine) IV infusion 15 mg/kg/h (up to 35 mg/kg/h in severe cases) indicated in severe symptoms, shock, metabolic acidosis, serum iron > 500 µg/dL or large ingestion; it binds free ferric iron forming ferrioxamine, excreted in urine imparting a reddish-brown 'vin rosé' colour; continue until symptoms resolve, acidosis corrects and urine colour normalises (usually < 24 h; prolonged infusion risks ARDS); adverse effects — hypotension with rapid infusion, ARDS, Yersinia sepsis, ototoxicity, retinal toxicity; oral desferrioxamine not recommended; deferasirox/deferiprone are used for chronic overload, not acute poisoning; (4) dialysis for renal failure (removes ferrioxamine); (5) prevention — child-proof containers, storing out of reach. Prognosis worst in stage III–IV.",
[["Clinical stages",[["Stage","Time","Features"],["I GI","0–6 h","Vomiting, haematemesis, diarrhoea"],["II Latent","6–24 h","Apparent recovery"],["III Shock","12–48 h","Shock, acidosis, coagulopathy, coma"],["IV Hepatic","2–5 days","Hepatic necrosis, hypoglycaemia"],["V Stricture","2–8 weeks","Pyloric stenosis"]]],
["Management",["Supportive: fluids, correct acidosis","Whole bowel irrigation (charcoal ineffective)","IV desferrioxamine 15 mg/kg/h","Watch 'vin rosé' urine","Avoid DFO > 24 h (ARDS)"]]],
{c:"Acute iron poisoning progresses through characteristic stages; early supportive care, decontamination and desferrioxamine chelation are life-saving."});

Z("pharm8",2,"ph",["Folic acid is a B vitamin needed for DNA synthesis; its deficiency causes megaloblastic anaemia and neural tube defects in the fetus, so it is given in pregnancy and in deficiency states.","Folate is the delivery van that brings one-carbon building blocks for DNA; without it, red cell precursors grow big but cannot divide (megaloblasts).",["Converted to tetrahydrofolate (THF) by DHFR; needed for thymidylate (dTMP) and purine synthesis, homocysteine → methionine (with B12).","Uses: megaloblastic anaemia (folate deficiency), prophylaxis in pregnancy (400 µg/day; 4–5 mg if previous NTD), haemolytic anaemias, malabsorption, drug-induced deficiency (phenytoin, methotrexate, trimethoprim), with iron (IFA tablets).","Folinic acid (leucovorin): active form, bypasses DHFR — methotrexate rescue, with 5-FU, pyrimethamine toxicity, methanol poisoning.","Caution: folic acid alone in B12 deficiency corrects anaemia but neurological damage (SACD) progresses — always check/give B12."],"Never give folate alone in B12 deficiency — 'blood gets better, nerves get worse'."],
"Folic acid (pteroylglutamic acid) is a water-soluble B-complex vitamin found in green leafy vegetables, liver, legumes and fruits; destroyed by prolonged cooking; body stores are small (5–20 mg; deficiency develops in 3–4 months). Daily requirement: 200 µg (adults), 400–600 µg in pregnancy. Absorption occurs in jejunum after deconjugation of polyglutamates; transported as methyl-THF. Biochemical role: reduced by dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) to tetrahydrofolate (THF), the carrier of one-carbon units, required for (1) synthesis of thymidylate from deoxyuridylate (thymidylate synthase — rate-limiting for DNA synthesis), (2) purine synthesis, (3) conversion of homocysteine to methionine (methyl-THF → THF, requires vitamin B12 — 'methylfolate trap' in B12 deficiency), (4) serine–glycine interconversion, histidine catabolism (FIGLU excretion test). Causes of deficiency: inadequate intake (poverty, alcoholism, elderly), increased requirement (pregnancy, lactation, infancy, haemolytic anaemia, malignancy, exfoliative dermatitis), malabsorption (celiac disease, tropical sprue, jejunal resection), drugs (phenytoin, phenobarbitone, primidone, methotrexate, trimethoprim, pyrimethamine, sulfasalazine, oral contraceptives, alcohol), dialysis. Effects of deficiency: megaloblastic anaemia (macrocytes, hypersegmented neutrophils, pancytopenia), glossitis, diarrhoea; neural tube defects in fetus; hyperhomocysteinaemia (vascular risk). Preparations: folic acid tablets 0.4, 1, 5 mg; injection; folinic acid (calcium leucovorin, 5-formyl-THF — does not require DHFR); L-methylfolate. Uses: (1) megaloblastic anaemia due to folate deficiency — 5 mg daily orally for 4 months; (2) prophylaxis in pregnancy — 400 µg daily from 1 month before conception through first trimester to prevent neural tube defects (5 mg in high-risk — previous NTD, epilepsy on AEDs, diabetes, obesity); iron–folic acid supplementation in National Anaemia Mukt Bharat programme; (3) chronic haemolytic anaemias (sickle cell, thalassaemia), (4) malabsorption, (5) prevention of drug-induced deficiency (phenytoin, methotrexate in rheumatoid arthritis — reduces toxicity), (6) dialysis patients, alcoholics, (7) hyperhomocysteinaemia. Folinic acid uses: methotrexate rescue after high-dose therapy and MTX toxicity, potentiation of 5-fluorouracil in colorectal cancer, pyrimethamine/trimethoprim toxicity, methanol poisoning, cerebral folate deficiency. Adverse effects: non-toxic; rarely allergic reactions; large doses may reduce efficacy of phenytoin (seizures). Important precaution: folic acid given alone to a patient with vitamin B12 deficiency corrects the haematological abnormality but does not prevent (and may precipitate) progression of subacute combined degeneration of spinal cord — B12 status must be assessed and B12 given along with folate in undiagnosed megaloblastic anaemia.",
[["Uses",["Folate deficiency megaloblastic anaemia","Pregnancy (prevent NTD): 400 µg; 5 mg high-risk","Haemolytic anaemias","Malabsorption, alcoholism","With phenytoin/methotrexate","Folinic acid: MTX rescue, 5-FU, methanol"]],
["Folic acid vs folinic acid",[["Feature","Folic acid","Folinic acid"],["Form","Requires DHFR to activate","Active (bypasses DHFR)"],["MTX toxicity","Ineffective","Antidote (rescue)"],["Main use","Deficiency, pregnancy","MTX rescue, 5-FU, methanol"]]]],
{c:"Folic acid prevents and treats megaloblastic anaemia and neural tube defects; folinic acid bypasses DHFR to rescue from antifolates, and B12 deficiency must always be excluded."});

Z("pharm8",3,"ph",["Vitamin K is a fat-soluble vitamin needed by the liver to activate clotting factors II, VII, IX and X; it treats bleeding due to vitamin K deficiency or warfarin overdose and is given to all newborns.","Vitamin K is the 'finishing tool' that adds a calcium-binding grip (carboxylation) to clotting factors; warfarin steals the tool.",["MOA: cofactor for γ-glutamyl carboxylase → carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX, X, protein C, S.","Forms: K1 phytonadione (plant; drug of choice, acts in 6–12 h), K2 menaquinones (bacteria), K3 menadione (synthetic; haemolysis/kernicterus in G6PD-deficient and newborns — avoid).","Uses: haemorrhagic disease of newborn prophylaxis (1 mg IM at birth; 0.5 mg preterm), warfarin overdose/raised INR, obstructive jaundice, malabsorption, prolonged antibiotics, salicylate/rodenticide poisoning.","ADR: anaphylactoid reactions with rapid IV (give slowly), haemolysis (menadione); for life-threatening warfarin bleeding use PCC/FFP plus IV vitamin K."],"Vitamin K-dependent factors: '1972' — X, IX, VII, II."],
"Vitamin K is a fat-soluble naphthoquinone. Forms: vitamin K1 (phytonadione/phylloquinone) — from green leafy vegetables; vitamin K2 (menaquinones) — synthesised by intestinal bacteria; vitamin K3 (menadione) — synthetic, water-soluble; requires bile for absorption (K1, K2). Daily requirement 50–100 µg; limited body stores. Mechanism: vitamin K hydroquinone (reduced form) is a cofactor for γ-glutamyl carboxylase in the liver, which carboxylates glutamic acid residues of precursor proteins to γ-carboxyglutamate, enabling Ca²⁺ binding and phospholipid attachment of clotting factors II (prothrombin), VII, IX, X and anticoagulant proteins C and S (also osteocalcin); during this reaction it is oxidised to vitamin K epoxide, which is recycled by vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKORC1) — the target of warfarin. Deficiency: newborns (sterile gut, poor placental transfer, low breast-milk content — haemorrhagic disease of the newborn/vitamin K deficiency bleeding), obstructive jaundice, malabsorption (celiac, cystic fibrosis), prolonged broad-spectrum antibiotics, liver disease (impaired synthesis — poor response), oral anticoagulants, poor dietary intake; causes prolonged PT/INR and bleeding. Uses: (1) prophylaxis of haemorrhagic disease of newborn — 1 mg IM at birth (0.5 mg in preterm < 1 kg) — universal recommendation; (2) treatment of vitamin K deficiency bleeding; (3) reversal of warfarin (coumarin) overdose or high INR — oral 1–5 mg for INR 9+ without bleeding; IV 5–10 mg slowly for serious bleeding along with 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate or fresh frozen plasma (vitamin K takes 6–24 h to act); (4) obstructive jaundice before surgery (parenteral), malabsorption syndromes, prolonged antibiotic therapy; (5) superwarfarin (rodenticide) poisoning — large doses for months; (6) salicylate poisoning with hypoprothrombinaemia. Not effective in heparin overdose (use protamine) or severe hepatocellular disease. Adverse effects: rapid IV injection can cause anaphylactoid reactions (flushing, dyspnoea, chest pain, hypotension, cardiovascular collapse) — give IV slowly (≤ 1 mg/min) diluted; menadione causes haemolysis and kernicterus in neonates and G6PD-deficient patients (not used); large doses may cause warfarin resistance for a week.",
[["Uses",["Prophylaxis of haemorrhagic disease of newborn (1 mg IM)","Warfarin overdose / high INR","Obstructive jaundice, malabsorption","Prolonged antibiotics","Rodenticide (superwarfarin), salicylate poisoning"]],
["Forms",[["Form","Source","Note"],["K1 (phytonadione)","Plants","Drug of choice"],["K2 (menaquinone)","Gut bacteria","Physiological"],["K3 (menadione)","Synthetic","Haemolysis, kernicterus — avoid in newborn"]]]],
{c:"Vitamin K enables carboxylation of clotting factors II, VII, IX and X; it is routinely given to newborns and is the specific antidote for warfarin, though slow acting, so serious bleeding also needs PCC or FFP."});

Z("pharm8",4,"ph",["Anticoagulants prevent formation and extension of blood clots: heparin acts immediately by activating antithrombin (given by injection), warfarin acts slowly by blocking vitamin K-dependent factor synthesis (oral), and LMWHs are more predictable injectable heparins.","Heparin is a fast-acting fire extinguisher (antithrombin booster); warfarin is a slow factory shutdown (vitamin K blocker); DOACs are direct blockers of thrombin or Xa.",["Classification: parenteral — UFH, LMWH (enoxaparin, dalteparin), fondaparinux, direct thrombin inhibitors (bivalirudin, argatroban); oral — coumarins (warfarin, acenocoumarol), indandiones, DOACs (dabigatran — thrombin; rivaroxaban, apixaban, edoxaban — Xa).","Heparin: binds antithrombin III → 1000× faster inactivation of IIa, Xa; monitor aPTT (1.5–2.5×); ADR — bleeding, HIT, osteoporosis, alopecia, hyperkalaemia; antidote — protamine sulfate.","Warfarin: inhibits VKOR (vitamin K epoxide reductase) → ↓ II, VII, IX, X; onset 3–5 days (overlap with heparin); monitor INR (2–3; 2.5–3.5 mechanical valves); ADR — bleeding, skin necrosis (protein C), teratogenic (fetal warfarin syndrome), many interactions; antidote — vitamin K, PCC, FFP.","LMWH: mainly anti-Xa, SC once/twice daily, predictable, no routine monitoring (anti-Xa if needed), less HIT and osteoporosis; renal excretion; partially reversed by protamine."],"Heparin → PTT (Heparin: 'int-PTT', intrinsic); Warfarin → PT (WEPT: Warfarin Extrinsic PT)."],
"Anticoagulants are drugs that reduce the coagulability of blood, used for prophylaxis and treatment of thromboembolism. Classification: (I) used in vivo — (A) parenteral: indirect thrombin inhibitors — unfractionated heparin (UFH), low molecular weight heparins (enoxaparin, dalteparin, tinzaparin, reviparin, nadroparin), fondaparinux (synthetic pentasaccharide), danaparoid; direct thrombin inhibitors — bivalirudin, argatroban, lepirudin; (B) oral: coumarin derivatives — warfarin, acenocoumarol (nicoumalone), dicoumarol, phenprocoumon; indandione — phenindione; direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs) — direct thrombin inhibitor dabigatran etexilate; direct factor Xa inhibitors rivaroxaban, apixaban, edoxaban; (II) used in vitro — heparin, sodium citrate, sodium oxalate, EDTA, sodium fluoride. Heparin: a mixture of sulphated mucopolysaccharides (MW 5,000–30,000) from mast cells, extracted from pig intestinal mucosa or bovine lung; strongest organic acid in body. Mechanism: binds via a unique pentasaccharide sequence to antithrombin III, inducing a conformational change that accelerates its inactivation of thrombin (IIa) and factor Xa (also IXa, XIa, XIIa) about 1000-fold; for thrombin inactivation heparin must bind both AT and thrombin (ternary complex; requires chains ≥ 18 saccharides), whereas Xa inactivation requires only AT binding; also releases lipoprotein lipase (clearing factor), inhibits platelet aggregation at high doses. Pharmacokinetics: not absorbed orally; given IV (immediate) or SC (onset 60 min); not given IM (haematoma); dose-dependent t½ 1–2 h; does not cross placenta (safe in pregnancy) or into milk; monitored by aPTT (maintain 1.5–2.5 times control) or anti-Xa; bedside — whole blood clotting time. Dose: IV bolus 80 U/kg then infusion 18 U/kg/h; prophylaxis 5000 U SC 8–12 hourly. Adverse effects: bleeding (most important), heparin-induced thrombocytopenia — type I (mild, non-immune, early) and type II (immune; IgG against heparin–PF4 complex, 5–14 days after starting; thrombocytopenia with paradoxical arterial and venous thrombosis; stop all heparins, give argatroban, bivalirudin or fondaparinux; avoid warfarin until platelets recover), osteoporosis and fractures (long-term), alopecia (reversible), hypersensitivity (animal origin), hyperkalaemia (aldosterone suppression), raised transaminases. Antidote: protamine sulphate (strongly basic protein from fish sperm; 1 mg neutralises 100 U heparin; given slowly IV; may itself cause anaphylaxis, hypotension, and anticoagulant effect in excess). Contraindications: bleeding disorders, haemophilia, thrombocytopenia, severe hypertension, intracranial haemorrhage, threatened abortion, peptic ulcer, piles, subacute bacterial endocarditis, recent surgery on eye/brain/spinal cord, lumbar puncture, regional anaesthesia, chronic alcoholics, cirrhosis, renal failure, aspirin/antiplatelets (caution). Low molecular weight heparins: obtained by depolymerisation (MW 3,000–7,000); chains too short to bridge AT and thrombin, so they selectively inhibit factor Xa (anti-Xa:anti-IIa ratio 2–4:1); advantages over UFH — high SC bioavailability (~90%), longer t½ (4 h) allowing once or twice daily SC dosing, predictable dose–response (weight-based) so routine laboratory monitoring is unnecessary (anti-Xa levels in renal failure, obesity, pregnancy), outpatient therapy possible, lower incidence of HIT and osteoporosis, less bleeding; disadvantages — renal excretion (accumulate in renal failure — use UFH if CrCl < 30), only partially reversed by protamine (~60%), more expensive; doses — enoxaparin 40 mg SC daily (prophylaxis), 1 mg/kg SC 12-hourly (treatment). Fondaparinux: synthetic pentasaccharide; selective indirect Xa inhibitor; once daily SC; no HIT; not reversed by protamine. Warfarin: 4-hydroxycoumarin; racemic mixture (S-warfarin more potent, metabolised by CYP2C9). Mechanism: inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKORC1), preventing regeneration of reduced vitamin K hydroquinone; the liver then synthesises non-carboxylated, inactive factors II, VII, IX and X (and proteins C and S). Since pre-formed factors must be cleared (half-lives: VII 6 h, IX 24 h, X 40 h, II 60 h), the anticoagulant effect develops over 3–5 days — so heparin is overlapped for at least 5 days and until INR is therapeutic for 24 h; early fall of protein C (short t½) creates a transient prothrombotic state. Pharmacokinetics: completely absorbed orally, 99% bound to albumin (displacement interactions), Vd small, metabolised by CYP2C9 (S) and CYP3A4/1A2 (R), t½ ~40 h, crosses placenta, minimal in milk (safe in lactation). Monitoring: prothrombin time expressed as International Normalised Ratio — target 2–3 for DVT/PE, AF, bioprosthetic valves; 2.5–3.5 for mechanical mitral valves; genetic polymorphisms (CYP2C9, VKORC1) influence dose. Adverse effects: bleeding (epistaxis, haematuria, GI, intracranial — most serious), cutaneous necrosis (days 3–8; protein C deficiency; fatty areas — breast, thighs), purple toe syndrome, alopecia, dermatitis, teratogenicity — fetal warfarin syndrome (nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses — chondrodysplasia punctata, CNS defects) in first trimester and fetal haemorrhage later — contraindicated in pregnancy (switch to LMWH). Drug interactions: potentiation (raised INR) — enzyme inhibitors (cimetidine, metronidazole, cotrimoxazole, fluconazole, amiodarone, erythromycin, ciprofloxacin, omeprazole, disulfiram, alcohol acutely), displacement from albumin (sulfonamides, phenylbutazone), reduced vitamin K synthesis (broad-spectrum antibiotics), antiplatelets and NSAIDs (pharmacodynamic bleeding), thyroxine; reduced effect — enzyme inducers (rifampicin, barbiturates, carbamazepine, phenytoin, griseofulvin, chronic alcohol), vitamin K-rich food, OCPs, cholestyramine (reduced absorption). Management of overdose: INR above range without bleeding — omit doses, adjust; INR > 9 — oral vitamin K 2.5–5 mg; serious/life-threatening bleeding — stop warfarin, IV vitamin K 10 mg slowly plus 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (or FFP). Uses of anticoagulants: deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism (treatment and prophylaxis after surgery, immobilisation), atrial fibrillation (stroke prevention — warfarin or DOAC), prosthetic heart valves (warfarin only), unstable angina/MI (heparin/LMWH), during PCI and cardiopulmonary bypass (heparin), haemodialysis, DIC (selected), peripheral arterial embolism, cerebral venous thrombosis, antiphospholipid syndrome (warfarin), pregnancy with thrombosis (LMWH). DOACs: fixed dosing, no routine monitoring, fewer interactions, less intracranial bleeding; reversal — idarucizumab (dabigatran), andexanet alfa (Xa inhibitors); contraindicated with mechanical valves and severe renal failure.",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs"],["UFH","Heparin"],["LMWH","Enoxaparin, dalteparin"],["Pentasaccharide","Fondaparinux"],["Direct thrombin inhibitors","Bivalirudin, argatroban (IV); dabigatran (oral)"],["Coumarins","Warfarin, acenocoumarol"],["Direct Xa inhibitors","Rivaroxaban, apixaban, edoxaban"],["In vitro","Citrate, oxalate, EDTA"]]],
["Heparin vs warfarin",[["Feature","Heparin","Warfarin"],["Route","IV, SC","Oral"],["Mechanism","Activates antithrombin → inactivates IIa, Xa","Inhibits VKOR → ↓ II, VII, IX, X synthesis"],["Onset","Immediate","3–5 days"],["Works in vitro","Yes","No"],["Monitoring","aPTT","PT/INR"],["Antidote","Protamine sulphate","Vitamin K, PCC, FFP"],["Pregnancy","Safe (doesn't cross placenta)","Teratogenic"],["Key ADR","Bleeding, HIT, osteoporosis","Bleeding, skin necrosis, interactions"]]],
["UFH vs LMWH",[["Feature","UFH","LMWH"],["Anti-Xa : anti-IIa","1:1","2–4:1"],["SC bioavailability","30%","90%"],["t½","1–2 h","4 h"],["Monitoring","aPTT","Usually none (anti-Xa)"],["HIT, osteoporosis","More","Less"],["Protamine reversal","Complete","Partial"],["Renal failure","Preferred","Accumulates"]]],
["Warfarin interactions",[["↑ Effect (bleeding)","↓ Effect"],["Enzyme inhibitors: cimetidine, metronidazole, fluconazole, amiodarone, cotrimoxazole","Enzyme inducers: rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine, barbiturates"],["Aspirin, NSAIDs, clopidogrel","Vitamin K, green vegetables"],["Broad-spectrum antibiotics","OCPs, cholestyramine"]]]],
{i:"Anticoagulants are the mainstay for prevention and treatment of venous thromboembolism and cardioembolic stroke; selection balances onset, route, monitoring and reversibility.",f:"Intrinsic (XII, XI, IX, VIII) + Extrinsic (VII, TF) → X → Xa → prothrombin (II) → thrombin → fibrin\n Heparin + AT → inactivates IIa, Xa (LMWH/fondaparinux: Xa)\n Warfarin → ↓ synthesis of II, VII, IX, X\n DOACs → direct Xa (rivaroxaban, apixaban) or IIa (dabigatran)",c:"Heparin provides immediate anticoagulation monitored by aPTT and reversed by protamine; warfarin provides oral long-term anticoagulation monitored by INR and reversed by vitamin K; LMWHs and DOACs offer predictable dosing without routine monitoring."});

Z("pharm8",5,"ph",["Streptokinase is a thrombolytic (fibrinolytic) drug from streptococci that activates plasminogen to plasmin, dissolving fresh clots; it is used in acute myocardial infarction, pulmonary embolism and DVT when PCI is not available.","Streptokinase recruits the body's own clot-dissolving enzyme (plasmin) — like hiring demolition workers for a fresh blockage.",["MOA: forms 1:1 complex with plasminogen → converts other plasminogen to plasmin → lyses fibrin (non-fibrin-specific → systemic lytic state).","Uses: STEMI within 12 h (1.5 MU IV over 1 h), massive PE, extensive DVT, peripheral arterial occlusion, blocked AV shunts.","ADR: bleeding (intracranial haemorrhage), allergic reactions/fever (antigenic), hypotension, reperfusion arrhythmias; antibodies → not reused for 1 year–lifelong.","CI: active bleeding, recent stroke/head injury/surgery, prior intracranial haemorrhage, aortic dissection, severe hypertension, recent streptococcal infection. Antidote: tranexamic acid, FFP/cryoprecipitate."],"Streptokinase is 'one-time use' — antibodies form."],
"Streptokinase (SK) is a non-enzymatic protein obtained from β-haemolytic streptococci (group C); it is a first-generation, non-fibrin-specific fibrinolytic agent widely used in India because of low cost. Mechanism: SK combines with plasminogen in a 1:1 stoichiometric complex that undergoes a conformational change exposing an active site; this activator complex converts free and fibrin-bound plasminogen to plasmin, which degrades fibrin (and fibrinogen, factors V and VIII) — producing a systemic lytic state. Pharmacokinetics: given IV; t½ about 30 min (shortened by antistreptococcal antibodies); antibodies develop within 5 days and persist for years, so it should not be repeated (resistance and allergy) — use alteplase/tenecteplase if re-lysis needed. Dose: acute MI — 1.5 million IU IV infusion over 60 minutes; PE/DVT — 250,000 IU loading over 30 min, then 100,000 IU/h for 24–72 h. Uses: (1) acute ST-elevation myocardial infarction — when primary PCI is not available within 120 min; maximum benefit within 3 hours, useful up to 12 hours (reduces mortality ~25%; given with aspirin and clopidogrel); (2) massive pulmonary embolism with haemodynamic instability; (3) extensive deep vein thrombosis (iliofemoral); (4) acute peripheral arterial thrombosis/embolism; (5) clotted arteriovenous shunts and catheters; (6) not used for acute ischaemic stroke (alteplase/tenecteplase preferred). Adverse effects: bleeding — at puncture sites, GI, genitourinary, intracranial haemorrhage (0.5–1%; most feared); allergic reactions — fever, chills, rashes, urticaria, rarely anaphylaxis (pretreat with hydrocortisone/antihistamine in some protocols); hypotension (bradykinin release — slow infusion); reperfusion arrhythmias (accelerated idioventricular rhythm — benign marker of reperfusion); serum sickness, Guillain–Barré (rare). Contraindications: absolute — prior intracranial haemorrhage, ischaemic stroke within 3 months, structural cerebral vascular lesion or intracranial neoplasm, active internal bleeding, suspected aortic dissection, significant head or facial trauma within 3 months, intracranial/spinal surgery within 2 months; relative — severe uncontrolled hypertension (> 180/110), recent major surgery (< 3 weeks), traumatic CPR, recent internal bleeding, pregnancy, active peptic ulcer, oral anticoagulant therapy, prior SK exposure (> 5 days earlier) or allergy, recent streptococcal infection. Management of bleeding: stop infusion, antifibrinolytic (tranexamic acid, epsilon-aminocaproic acid), fresh frozen plasma, cryoprecipitate (fibrinogen), blood transfusion. Other fibrinolytics: urokinase, alteplase (rt-PA; fibrin-specific), reteplase, tenecteplase (single bolus; most fibrin-specific).",
[["Streptokinase vs alteplase",[["Feature","Streptokinase","Alteplase (tPA)"],["Source","Streptococcus","Recombinant human"],["Fibrin specificity","No (systemic lysis)","Yes"],["Antigenic","Yes (not repeated)","No"],["Hypotension","Yes","Rare"],["Cost","Low","High"],["Stroke use","No","Yes"]]],
["Contraindications",["Prior intracranial haemorrhage","Ischaemic stroke < 3 months","Active bleeding","Aortic dissection","Recent head trauma/neurosurgery","Severe uncontrolled hypertension"]]],
{c:"Streptokinase is an inexpensive fibrinolytic that saves lives in STEMI when given early; its antigenicity and non-specific systemic lysis limit repeat use and increase bleeding risk."});

Z("pharm8",6,"ph",["Antiplatelet drugs prevent platelets from clumping together to form arterial clots; aspirin (COX inhibitor) and clopidogrel (P2Y12 ADP-receptor blocker) are the most widely used, especially to prevent heart attack and stroke.","Platelets are the first responders that stick together at a damaged artery; antiplatelets stop them calling each other (aspirin blocks TXA2 signal; clopidogrel blocks ADP signal).",["Classification: COX inhibitor (aspirin), P2Y12 antagonists (clopidogrel, prasugrel, ticagrelor, ticlopidine, cangrelor), PDE inhibitors (dipyridamole, cilostazol), GP IIb/IIIa antagonists (abciximab, eptifibatide, tirofiban), PAR-1 antagonist (vorapaxar).","Aspirin: irreversibly acetylates platelet COX-1 → ↓ TXA2 for platelet life (7–10 days); low dose 75–150 mg spares endothelial PGI2. ADR — GI bleeding/ulcer, bleeding, asthma, Reye's; resistance.","Clopidogrel: prodrug activated by CYP2C19 → irreversible P2Y12 blockade; ADR — bleeding, diarrhoea, rash, TTP (rare); poor metabolisers, interaction with omeprazole.","Uses: ACS (DAPT), post-PCI stenting, secondary prevention of MI/stroke/TIA, peripheral arterial disease, AF (when anticoagulants contraindicated), pre-eclampsia prophylaxis (aspirin)."],"DAPT = Dual AntiPlatelet Therapy: Aspirin + Clopidogrel/Ticagrelor."],
"Antiplatelet drugs interfere with platelet adhesion, activation and aggregation, which are central to arterial thrombosis (white thrombus) over ruptured atherosclerotic plaques. Classification: (1) COX inhibitor — aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid); (2) P2Y12 (ADP) receptor antagonists — thienopyridines: ticlopidine, clopidogrel, prasugrel (irreversible prodrugs); non-thienopyridines: ticagrelor (reversible, oral, direct-acting), cangrelor (IV); (3) phosphodiesterase inhibitors — dipyridamole, cilostazol; (4) GP IIb/IIIa receptor antagonists — abciximab, eptifibatide, tirofiban (IV); (5) PAR-1 (thrombin receptor) antagonist — vorapaxar; (6) others — prostacyclin analogues (epoprostenol), omega-3 fatty acids. Aspirin: mechanism — irreversibly acetylates serine 529 of COX-1 in platelets, blocking synthesis of thromboxane A2 (potent aggregator and vasoconstrictor); since platelets lack nuclei and cannot synthesise new enzyme, inhibition lasts for the platelet lifespan (7–10 days); endothelial cells regenerate COX and continue producing prostacyclin (PGI2 — antiaggregatory) — low doses (75–150 mg/day) achieve selective platelet effect (aspirin dilemma); a loading dose 162–325 mg chewed is used in acute coronary syndromes. Uses — secondary prevention of MI, stroke and TIA (reduces risk ~25%); acute coronary syndrome (immediately); after CABG and PCI; stable angina; peripheral vascular disease; atrial fibrillation in low-risk patients (limited); prevention of pre-eclampsia in high-risk pregnancy (from 12 weeks); Kawasaki disease; primary prevention only in selected high-risk persons (bleeding risk outweighs benefit for most); colorectal cancer prevention (Lynch syndrome). Adverse effects — gastric irritation, peptic ulceration, GI bleeding (dose-related; with PPI cover in high risk), increased bleeding time, haemorrhagic stroke (small risk), aspirin-induced asthma (aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease — leukotriene shunting), hypersensitivity, Reye syndrome in children with viral infections, 'aspirin resistance'; interactions — ibuprofen interferes with aspirin's access to COX-1 (take aspirin first), anticoagulants (bleeding). Clopidogrel: thienopyridine prodrug; two-step activation by hepatic CYP2C19 (and 3A4, 1A2) to an active thiol metabolite that irreversibly binds the P2Y12 ADP receptor, preventing ADP-induced activation of GP IIb/IIIa receptors and aggregation; onset in hours (faster with 300–600 mg loading), effect persists 5–7 days after stopping (stop 5 days before surgery); maintenance 75 mg daily. Uses — dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) with aspirin in acute coronary syndromes (12 months), after PCI with stenting (bare metal 1 month; drug-eluting 6–12 months), secondary prevention in recent MI, ischaemic stroke and peripheral arterial disease (alternative to aspirin, slightly more effective — CAPRIE), aspirin intolerance, short-term DAPT after minor stroke/TIA. Adverse effects — bleeding, dyspepsia, diarrhoea, rash, rarely neutropenia and thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (less than ticlopidine); variable response — CYP2C19 poor metabolisers (common in Asians) have reduced activation and more stent thrombosis (use ticagrelor/prasugrel); omeprazole and esomeprazole inhibit CYP2C19 and reduce efficacy (pantoprazole preferred). Ticagrelor (reversible, more potent, faster; dyspnoea, bradycardia) and prasugrel (more potent; bleeding; contraindicated after stroke) are preferred in ACS. Dipyridamole (adenosine reuptake inhibition, PDE inhibition) with aspirin for stroke prevention; cilostazol (PDE3) for intermittent claudication. GP IIb/IIIa antagonists block the final common pathway of aggregation (see separate topic).",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs","Mechanism"],["COX inhibitor","Aspirin","Irreversible COX-1 → ↓ TXA2"],["P2Y12 antagonists","Clopidogrel, prasugrel, ticagrelor, cangrelor","Block ADP receptor"],["PDE inhibitors","Dipyridamole, cilostazol","↑ cAMP"],["GP IIb/IIIa antagonists","Abciximab, eptifibatide, tirofiban","Block fibrinogen binding"],["PAR-1 antagonist","Vorapaxar","Block thrombin receptor"]]],
["Aspirin vs clopidogrel",[["Feature","Aspirin","Clopidogrel"],["Target","COX-1 (TXA2)","P2Y12 (ADP)"],["Activation","Active drug","Prodrug (CYP2C19)"],["Reversibility","Irreversible","Irreversible"],["Dose","75–150 mg (load 325)","75 mg (load 300–600)"],["Key ADR","GI ulcer/bleed, asthma, Reye's","Bleeding, diarrhoea, TTP (rare)"],["Interaction","Ibuprofen, anticoagulants","Omeprazole (↓ activation)"],["Stop before surgery","7 days","5 days"]]],
["Uses",["Acute coronary syndrome (DAPT)","After PCI/stenting, CABG","Secondary prevention of MI, stroke, TIA","Peripheral arterial disease","Pre-eclampsia prophylaxis (aspirin)","Kawasaki disease (aspirin)"]]],
{i:"Platelet aggregation is central to arterial thrombosis; antiplatelet therapy reduces cardiovascular events by about a quarter in high-risk patients.",f:"Vessel injury → platelet adhesion (vWF–GPIb)\n → activation: TXA2 (←aspirin), ADP–P2Y12 (←clopidogrel), thrombin–PAR1 (←vorapaxar)\n → GP IIb/IIIa activation (←abciximab) → fibrinogen bridges → aggregation → thrombus",c:"Aspirin and clopidogrel block complementary platelet activation pathways; their combination (DAPT) is standard after ACS and stenting, balancing ischaemic protection against bleeding risk."});

Z("pharm8",7,"ph",["GP IIb/IIIa receptor antagonists (abciximab, eptifibatide, tirofiban) are the most potent antiplatelet drugs; they block the final common pathway of platelet aggregation and are given IV during high-risk PCI.","Whatever the trigger, platelets finally link arms through GP IIb/IIIa receptors holding fibrinogen; these drugs cut the arms.",["MOA: block GP IIb/IIIa (αIIbβ3 integrin) → prevent fibrinogen/vWF binding → inhibit aggregation from all agonists.","Abciximab: chimeric Fab monoclonal antibody, irreversible-like, long platelet effect (24–48 h); eptifibatide (cyclic heptapeptide) and tirofiban (non-peptide) — reversible, short-acting, renal excretion.","Uses: high-risk PCI (large thrombus, no-reflow), NSTE-ACS with ongoing ischaemia (bailout), with heparin and aspirin.","ADR: bleeding, thrombocytopenia (esp. abciximab), hypotension; CI — recent bleeding/stroke, surgery, thrombocytopenia; oral forms failed (↑ mortality)."],"GP IIb/IIIa = 'final common pathway' blockers."],
"Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa (integrin αIIbβ3) is the most abundant platelet surface receptor; upon activation by any agonist (ADP, thrombin, TXA2, collagen, adrenaline), it undergoes conformational change and binds fibrinogen and von Willebrand factor, cross-linking adjacent platelets — the final common pathway of aggregation. GP IIb/IIIa antagonists block this receptor and therefore inhibit aggregation irrespective of the stimulus — the most potent antiplatelet agents. Drugs: (1) abciximab — chimeric (mouse–human) monoclonal antibody Fab fragment against GP IIb/IIIa (also binds vitronectin receptor); given IV bolus + infusion for 12 h; plasma t½ short but platelet-bound effect persists 24–48 h (functionally irreversible); reversed by platelet transfusion; not renally cleared; withdrawn in many markets; (2) eptifibatide — cyclic heptapeptide based on KGD sequence (from snake venom); reversible, competitive; t½ 2.5 h; renal excretion (dose reduction); (3) tirofiban — non-peptide tyrosine derivative mimicking RGD; reversible; t½ 2 h; renal excretion. Uses: adjunct to PCI in high-risk patients (large thrombus burden, slow/no-reflow, bailout for thrombotic complications), particularly in STEMI not pretreated with P2Y12 inhibitors; NSTE-ACS with refractory ischaemia (upstream use no longer routine); given with aspirin and heparin (reduced heparin dose). Adverse effects: bleeding (major and minor, access site), thrombocytopenia (abciximab 1–2%, acute profound — monitor platelets at 2–4 h and 24 h), pseudothrombocytopenia (EDTA), hypotension, hypersensitivity, antibody formation (abciximab). Contraindications: active bleeding, history of stroke within 30 days or any haemorrhagic stroke, major surgery or trauma within 6 weeks, severe uncontrolled hypertension, thrombocytopenia < 100,000/µL, aortic dissection, severe renal failure (eptifibatide, tirofiban), concurrent other GP IIb/IIIa antagonist. Oral GP IIb/IIIa antagonists (xemilofiban, orbofiban, sibrafiban) increased mortality and bleeding and were abandoned.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Abciximab","Eptifibatide","Tirofiban"],["Nature","Monoclonal Fab","Cyclic peptide","Non-peptide"],["Binding","Functionally irreversible","Reversible","Reversible"],["Platelet effect","24–48 h","4–8 h","4–8 h"],["Excretion","Proteolysis","Renal","Renal"],["Thrombocytopenia","More","Less","Less"]]],
["Uses",["High-risk PCI (thrombus, no-reflow)","NSTE-ACS with refractory ischaemia","Bailout during stenting"]]],
{c:"GP IIb/IIIa antagonists block the final common pathway of platelet aggregation; as IV agents used during high-risk PCI they reduce ischaemic events but at the cost of bleeding and thrombocytopenia."});

Z("pharm8",8,"ph",["Hypolipidaemic drugs lower blood cholesterol and triglycerides to prevent atherosclerosis; statins (atorvastatin, rosuvastatin) are the most effective, lowering LDL by inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase.","Statins switch off the liver's cholesterol factory; the liver then pulls more LDL from blood by making more LDL receptors.",["Classification: statins (atorvastatin, rosuvastatin, simvastatin), bile acid sequestrants (cholestyramine, colesevelam), cholesterol absorption inhibitor (ezetimibe), fibrates (fenofibrate, gemfibrozil), niacin, PCSK9 inhibitors (evolocumab, alirocumab, inclisiran), bempedoic acid, omega-3 fatty acids, lomitapide.","Statin MOA: competitive inhibition of HMG-CoA reductase (rate-limiting) → ↓ hepatic cholesterol → ↑ LDL receptor expression → ↓ LDL 20–60%; also ↓ TG, ↑ HDL; pleiotropic — plaque stabilisation, anti-inflammatory, ↓ CRP.","Uses: primary hypercholesterolaemia, mixed dyslipidaemia, primary & secondary prevention of CAD/stroke (ACS — high-intensity atorvastatin 40–80 mg), diabetes (> 40 yr), familial hypercholesterolaemia.","ADR: myalgia, myopathy, rhabdomyolysis (↑ with fibrates esp. gemfibrozil, macrolides, azoles, cyclosporine), raised transaminases, new-onset diabetes, GI upset; CI — pregnancy, active liver disease; given at night (short-acting statins)."],"Statins 'HMG-CoA reductase' — give at bedtime (cholesterol synthesis peaks at night)."],
"Hyperlipidaemia (raised LDL cholesterol, triglycerides or reduced HDL) is a major risk factor for atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease. Classification of hypolipidaemic drugs: (1) HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors (statins) — lovastatin, simvastatin, pravastatin, fluvastatin, atorvastatin, rosuvastatin, pitavastatin; (2) bile acid sequestrants (resins) — cholestyramine, colestipol, colesevelam; (3) cholesterol absorption inhibitor — ezetimibe (blocks NPC1L1 transporter); (4) activators of lipoprotein lipase (fibric acid derivatives, PPAR-α agonists) — fenofibrate, gemfibrozil, bezafibrate, saroglitazar (PPAR α/γ — India); (5) lipolysis and triglyceride synthesis inhibitor — nicotinic acid (niacin); (6) PCSK9 inhibitors — evolocumab, alirocumab (monoclonal antibodies), inclisiran (siRNA); (7) ATP-citrate lyase inhibitor — bempedoic acid; (8) others — omega-3 fatty acids (icosapent ethyl), lomitapide (MTP inhibitor), mipomersen, evinacumab (HoFH). Statins: mechanism — structural analogues of HMG-CoA; competitively inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis (HMG-CoA → mevalonate); reduced intracellular cholesterol in hepatocytes activates SREBP-2, upregulating LDL receptor synthesis, increasing clearance of LDL and VLDL remnants from plasma; also reduce hepatic VLDL secretion; effects — LDL reduction 20–60% (dose-dependent; doubling dose gives additional 6% — 'rule of 6'), TG reduction 10–30%, HDL increase 5–10%; pleiotropic effects — improved endothelial function (NO), plaque stabilisation (reduced lipid core, macrophages and MMPs), anti-inflammatory (reduce CRP), antithrombotic, immunomodulatory. Pharmacokinetics: lovastatin and simvastatin are lactone prodrugs; high first-pass hepatic extraction (target organ); metabolised by CYP3A4 (atorvastatin, simvastatin, lovastatin — interactions) or CYP2C9 (fluvastatin, rosuvastatin minor); pravastatin and rosuvastatin are hydrophilic; short t½ statins (simvastatin, lovastatin) given at bedtime (peak cholesterol synthesis at night); atorvastatin (t½ 14 h; active metabolites 20–30 h) and rosuvastatin (19 h) any time. Intensity: high-intensity (≥ 50% LDL fall) — atorvastatin 40–80 mg, rosuvastatin 20–40 mg; moderate (30–49%) — atorvastatin 10–20 mg, rosuvastatin 5–10 mg, simvastatin 20–40 mg. Uses: (1) primary hypercholesterolaemia and mixed dyslipidaemia; (2) secondary prevention — established coronary heart disease, after ACS (high-intensity early, regardless of baseline LDL), ischaemic stroke/TIA, peripheral arterial disease — reduce cardiovascular events and mortality; (3) primary prevention in high-risk individuals — diabetes aged 40–75, LDL ≥ 190 mg/dL, high 10-year ASCVD risk, CKD; (4) familial hypercholesterolaemia (with ezetimibe, PCSK9 inhibitors); targets — LDL < 55 mg/dL (very high risk), < 70 (high risk), < 100 (moderate). Adverse effects: generally well tolerated; GI disturbances, headache, rash, sleep disturbance; statin-associated muscle symptoms — myalgia (5–10%), myositis, myopathy (CK > 10 × ULN), rarely rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria and acute renal failure — risk increased by high dose, elderly, hypothyroidism, renal/hepatic impairment, and interacting drugs (gemfibrozil, niacin, CYP3A4 inhibitors — macrolides, azoles, HIV protease inhibitors, cyclosporine, diltiazem, verapamil, amiodarone, grapefruit juice); elevation of hepatic transaminases (> 3 × ULN in 1%; check LFT baseline), rarely hepatitis; modest increase in new-onset diabetes (benefit outweighs risk); immune-mediated necrotising myopathy (anti-HMGCR); teratogenic — contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation; active liver disease. Atorvastatin: potent, long-acting statin (10–80 mg once daily any time), metabolised by CYP3A4, active metabolites; widely used in ACS (80 mg — PROVE-IT, MIRACL trials), primary and secondary prevention, diabetes. Other drugs briefly: ezetimibe (add-on, LDL ↓ 20%; well tolerated); fibrates (TG ↓ 30–50%, HDL ↑; drug of choice for severe hypertriglyceridaemia > 500 mg/dL to prevent pancreatitis; gallstones, myopathy with statins — fenofibrate safer than gemfibrozil); niacin (raises HDL most; flushing — aspirin pretreatment, hyperglycaemia, hyperuricaemia, hepatotoxicity; outcome benefit not shown); bile acid sequestrants (safe in pregnancy and children; GI side effects, raise TG, bind drugs); PCSK9 inhibitors (LDL ↓ 50–60% additional; SC injection every 2–4 weeks; for FH and very high-risk patients).",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs","Main effect"],["Statins","Atorvastatin, rosuvastatin, simvastatin","↓↓ LDL"],["Ezetimibe","Ezetimibe","↓ LDL (absorption)"],["Bile acid sequestrants","Cholestyramine, colesevelam","↓ LDL; ↑ TG"],["Fibrates","Fenofibrate, gemfibrozil","↓↓ TG, ↑ HDL"],["Niacin","Nicotinic acid","↑↑ HDL, ↓ TG, ↓ LDL"],["PCSK9 inhibitors","Evolocumab, alirocumab, inclisiran","↓↓ LDL"],["Others","Bempedoic acid, omega-3, lomitapide","Varied"]]],
["Statins: summary",[["Aspect","Detail"],["MOA","HMG-CoA reductase inhibition → ↑ LDL receptors"],["LDL lowering","20–60%"],["Pleiotropic","Plaque stabilisation, ↓ CRP, endothelial function"],["Key ADR","Myopathy, rhabdomyolysis, ↑ ALT, new diabetes"],["Interactions","Gemfibrozil, macrolides, azoles, cyclosporine, grapefruit"],["CI","Pregnancy, lactation, active liver disease"]]],
["Uses of statins",["Hypercholesterolaemia, mixed dyslipidaemia","Secondary prevention: CAD, ACS, stroke, PAD","Primary prevention: diabetes, high-risk","Familial hypercholesterolaemia"]]],
{i:"Lowering LDL cholesterol reduces cardiovascular events proportionally; statins are the cornerstone, with ezetimibe and PCSK9 inhibitors added to reach targets.",f:"Acetyl-CoA → HMG-CoA → (HMG-CoA reductase ← statins) → mevalonate → cholesterol\n ↓ Hepatocyte cholesterol → SREBP-2 → ↑ LDL receptors → ↑ LDL clearance\n Ezetimibe: ↓ gut absorption (NPC1L1); PCSK9 inhibitors: ↓ LDL-R degradation",c:"Statins such as atorvastatin are the most effective and evidence-based lipid-lowering drugs; monitoring for myopathy and hepatotoxicity and avoidance of interacting drugs ensure safe use."});

Z("pharm9",0,"ph",["Peptic ulcer is treated by reducing gastric acid (PPIs, H2 blockers), protecting the mucosa, eradicating H. pylori and stopping NSAIDs; proton pump inhibitors (omeprazole) are the most effective acid suppressants.","PPIs switch off the final pump (H⁺/K⁺-ATPase) that pours acid into the stomach — regardless of which signal (histamine, gastrin, acetylcholine) turned it on.",["Drugs: PPIs (omeprazole, pantoprazole, esomeprazole, rabeprazole, lansoprazole), H2 blockers (famotidine, ranitidine), antacids (Mg/Al hydroxide), mucosal protectives (sucralfate, bismuth, misoprostol), anti-H. pylori regimens, P-CABs (vonoprazan).","Omeprazole MOA: prodrug activated in acidic canaliculi to sulfenamide → covalent (irreversible) binding to H⁺/K⁺-ATPase → ↓ acid ~90%; give 30 min before breakfast; effect lasts 24–48 h.","Uses: peptic ulcer (4–8 weeks), H. pylori eradication, GERD (drug of choice), Zollinger–Ellison, NSAID ulcer prophylaxis, stress ulcer prophylaxis, upper GI bleeding (IV), dyspepsia.","ADR: headache, diarrhoea, nausea; long-term — B12 deficiency, hypomagnesaemia, osteoporosis/fractures, C. difficile, pneumonia, iron malabsorption, rebound hypersecretion; interactions — ↓ clopidogrel (CYP2C19), ↓ ketoconazole absorption."],"PPI 'Pump is Permanently Inhibited' — take before meals."],
"Peptic ulcer disease (gastric and duodenal ulcers) results from imbalance between aggressive factors (acid, pepsin, H. pylori, NSAIDs, smoking, bile) and defensive factors (mucus, bicarbonate, prostaglandins, mucosal blood flow, epithelial renewal). Treatment goals: relieve pain, heal ulcer, prevent complications and recurrence. Drugs: (1) reduction of gastric acid secretion — proton pump inhibitors (omeprazole, esomeprazole, lansoprazole, pantoprazole, rabeprazole, dexlansoprazole), potassium-competitive acid blockers (vonoprazan), H2 receptor antagonists (cimetidine, ranitidine — withdrawn for NDMA, famotidine, roxatidine), anticholinergics (pirenzepine — M1, obsolete), prostaglandin analogue (misoprostol); (2) neutralisation of acid — antacids: systemic (sodium bicarbonate), non-systemic (magnesium hydroxide, magnesium trisilicate, aluminium hydroxide, calcium carbonate, magaldrate) — symptomatic relief; (3) ulcer protectives — sucralfate, colloidal bismuth subcitrate; (4) ulcer healing promoters — carbenoxolone (obsolete); (5) anti-H. pylori drugs — amoxicillin, clarithromycin, metronidazole, tinidazole, tetracycline, levofloxacin, bismuth, PPI. Proton pump inhibitors — omeprazole: substituted benzimidazole; inactive prodrug and weak base; absorbed and reaches parietal cells via blood; accumulates in the acidic secretory canaliculi (pH < 4), where it is protonated and converted to an active tetracyclic sulfenamide which forms covalent disulphide bonds with cysteine residues of H⁺/K⁺-ATPase (proton pump) on the luminal surface, irreversibly inactivating it; acid secretion resumes only after synthesis of new pumps (t½ of effect 24–48 h despite plasma t½ 1–1.5 h); inhibits both basal and stimulated secretion (final common pathway) by 80–95%; maximal effect after 3–4 days of daily dosing; best given 30–60 min before breakfast (pumps activated by food); acid-labile — enteric-coated granules; metabolised by CYP2C19 and 3A4 (genetic polymorphism — poor metabolisers respond better); IV preparations (pantoprazole, esomeprazole) for bleeding. Uses: (1) peptic ulcer — duodenal ulcer healing in 4 weeks (90%+), gastric ulcer 8 weeks (omeprazole 20–40 mg/day); (2) H. pylori eradication as part of triple/quadruple therapy; (3) gastro-oesophageal reflux disease — drug of choice for erosive oesophagitis, maintenance; Barrett oesophagus; (4) Zollinger–Ellison syndrome (high doses 60–120 mg/day); (5) prophylaxis and treatment of NSAID-induced ulcers; (6) stress ulcer prophylaxis in ICU; (7) upper GI bleeding from peptic ulcer — high-dose IV PPI before and after endoscopy (reduces rebleeding); (8) functional dyspepsia; (9) aspiration pneumonitis prophylaxis before anaesthesia; (10) with pancreatic enzyme replacement. Adverse effects: short-term — headache, nausea, abdominal pain, diarrhoea, flatulence, constipation, dizziness, rashes; long-term — hypergastrinaemia (rebound acid hypersecretion on stopping; ECL cell hyperplasia, carcinoid in rats), vitamin B12 deficiency, iron malabsorption, hypomagnesaemia (with hypocalcaemia, hypokalaemia), increased risk of hip, wrist and spine fractures (calcium malabsorption), enteric infections (Clostridioides difficile, Salmonella, Campylobacter), community-acquired pneumonia, small intestinal bacterial overgrowth, acute and chronic interstitial nephritis, subacute cutaneous lupus, fundic gland polyps, possible association with dementia and CKD; gynaecomastia rare. Drug interactions: reduced absorption of drugs requiring acid (ketoconazole, itraconazole, atazanavir, iron salts, dasatinib); omeprazole and esomeprazole inhibit CYP2C19 — reduce activation of clopidogrel (pantoprazole preferred), raise diazepam, phenytoin, warfarin levels; increase methotrexate toxicity. Treatment regimen for peptic ulcer: H. pylori positive — eradication therapy followed by PPI for 4–8 weeks; H. pylori negative/NSAID-related — stop NSAID if possible, PPI 8 weeks; if NSAID must continue — PPI co-therapy or COX-2 selective drug with PPI; lifestyle — stop smoking, avoid alcohol; surgery for complications (perforation, obstruction, refractory bleeding).",
[["Drugs for peptic ulcer",[["Group","Drugs"],["PPIs","Omeprazole, pantoprazole, esomeprazole, rabeprazole, lansoprazole"],["P-CAB","Vonoprazan"],["H2 blockers","Famotidine, cimetidine, (ranitidine)"],["Antacids","Mg(OH)2, Al(OH)3, CaCO3, NaHCO3"],["Mucosal protectives","Sucralfate, bismuth subcitrate"],["PG analogue","Misoprostol"],["Anti-H. pylori","Amoxicillin, clarithromycin, metronidazole, tetracycline, levofloxacin"]]],
["PPI vs H2 blocker",[["Feature","PPI","H2 blocker"],["Target","H⁺/K⁺-ATPase (final step)","H2 receptor"],["Binding","Irreversible","Competitive"],["Acid suppression","80–95%","50–70%"],["Meal-stimulated secretion","Blocked","Partially"],["Tolerance","No","Yes"],["Healing rate","Faster, higher","Lower"]]],
["Long-term PPI adverse effects",["B12 and iron deficiency","Hypomagnesaemia","Fractures","C. difficile, pneumonia","Interstitial nephritis","Rebound hypersecretion"]]],
{i:"Acid suppression with PPIs and eradication of H. pylori have made peptic ulcer largely a medical disease, with surgery reserved for complications.",f:"Histamine (H2) / Gastrin (CCK2) / ACh (M3) → parietal cell\n → H⁺/K⁺-ATPase (proton pump) → acid\n PPI → activated in canaliculi → covalent binding → irreversible block\n H2 blocker → blocks histamine input only",c:"Omeprazole and other PPIs are the most potent acid suppressants and the backbone of peptic ulcer therapy; long-term use requires attention to deficiencies, infections and interactions."});

Z("pharm9",1,"ph",["H. pylori eradication uses a combination of a PPI plus two or more antibiotics (and sometimes bismuth) for 10–14 days; eradication heals ulcers and prevents recurrence.","H. pylori hides under the mucus in its acid-neutralising cloud; we lower acid with a PPI so antibiotics work, and use two or three to prevent resistance.",["Triple therapy (14 days): PPI + amoxicillin 1 g BD + clarithromycin 500 mg BD (or metronidazole if penicillin allergy) — only if low clarithromycin resistance.","Bismuth quadruple (10–14 days): PPI + bismuth + tetracycline + metronidazole — first-line in high clarithromycin resistance (India), penicillin allergy.","Others: concomitant (PPI + amoxicillin + clarithromycin + metronidazole), sequential, levofloxacin-based, rifabutin-based, vonoprazan dual/triple; high-dose dual (PPI + amoxicillin).","Confirm eradication by urea breath test or stool antigen ≥ 4 weeks after therapy (off PPI 2 weeks); failure due to resistance, poor compliance."],"Triple therapy 'PAC': PPI + Amoxicillin + Clarithromycin."],
"Helicobacter pylori is a Gram-negative, microaerophilic, spiral, urease-positive bacillus colonising gastric mucosa; it causes chronic gastritis, duodenal ulcer (90%), gastric ulcer (70%), gastric adenocarcinoma and MALT lymphoma. Eradication cures most ulcers and prevents recurrence (from 60–80% per year to < 10%). Why combination therapy: single antibiotics fail because of rapid resistance, poor penetration into mucus layer and acid inactivation; PPIs raise gastric pH (increasing antibiotic stability and activity — amoxicillin and clarithromycin are acid-labile), have weak anti-H. pylori effect, and heal the ulcer. Drugs used: amoxicillin (resistance rare), clarithromycin (resistance increasing, > 15% in many regions including India), metronidazole/tinidazole (high resistance in India but partly overcome by higher dose), tetracycline, levofloxacin, rifabutin, furazolidone, bismuth subcitrate/subsalicylate (direct antibacterial, disrupts cell wall, prevents adhesion; black stools/tongue), PPIs (omeprazole 20 mg BD, esomeprazole 40 mg BD), vonoprazan (potassium-competitive acid blocker — more potent). Regimens: (1) standard clarithromycin triple therapy — PPI BD + amoxicillin 1 g BD + clarithromycin 500 mg BD × 14 days (or metronidazole 500 mg BD instead of amoxicillin in penicillin allergy); recommended only where clarithromycin resistance < 15% or known susceptibility; (2) bismuth quadruple therapy — PPI BD + bismuth subcitrate 120 mg QID (or 240 mg BD) + tetracycline 500 mg QID + metronidazole 400–500 mg TDS–QID × 10–14 days; first-line where clarithromycin resistance high; salvage regimen; (3) non-bismuth quadruple (concomitant) — PPI + amoxicillin + clarithromycin + metronidazole × 14 days; (4) sequential therapy — PPI + amoxicillin × 5 days, then PPI + clarithromycin + metronidazole × 5 days; (5) hybrid therapy; (6) levofloxacin triple — PPI + amoxicillin + levofloxacin 500 mg OD × 10–14 days (second-line); (7) rifabutin triple — rescue; (8) high-dose dual therapy — PPI (or vonoprazan) + amoxicillin 3 g/day; (9) vonoprazan-based dual/triple therapy (newer). After eradication: continue PPI for 4–8 weeks in complicated or gastric ulcer; test of cure ≥ 4 weeks after antibiotics and ≥ 2 weeks after stopping PPI — urea breath test or stool antigen (serology unsuitable); culture and susceptibility-guided therapy after two failures. Adverse effects: diarrhoea, nausea, metallic taste (metronidazole, clarithromycin), disulfiram reaction (metronidazole with alcohol), black stools (bismuth), antibiotic-associated colitis, allergic reactions; compliance is crucial.",
[["Regimens",[["Regimen","Drugs","Duration"],["Clarithromycin triple","PPI + amoxicillin + clarithromycin","14 days"],["Bismuth quadruple","PPI + bismuth + tetracycline + metronidazole","10–14 days"],["Concomitant","PPI + amoxicillin + clarithromycin + metronidazole","14 days"],["Levofloxacin triple","PPI + amoxicillin + levofloxacin","10–14 days"],["High-dose dual","PPI/vonoprazan + amoxicillin","14 days"],["Rifabutin triple","PPI + amoxicillin + rifabutin","Rescue"]]],
["Role of each component",["PPI: ↑ pH → antibiotic stability, ulcer healing","Amoxicillin: low resistance","Clarithromycin: potent but resistance rising","Metronidazole/tinidazole: anaerobic, resistance common","Bismuth: direct antibacterial, anti-adhesion"]]],
{c:"H. pylori eradication requires combination therapy with a PPI and antibiotics chosen according to local resistance; confirming cure prevents ulcer recurrence and reduces cancer risk."});

Z("pharm9",2,"ph",["GERD (gastro-oesophageal reflux disease) is treated with lifestyle changes and acid suppression; PPIs are the most effective drugs, with H2 blockers, antacids/alginates and prokinetics as additions.","Acid escapes upwards through a weak valve (LES); we reduce the acid with PPIs, and keep it down with lifestyle and alginate rafts.",["Lifestyle: weight loss, elevate head of bed, avoid late meals, fatty food, chocolate, caffeine, alcohol, smoking; avoid drugs that relax LES (CCBs, nitrates, anticholinergics, theophylline).","PPIs (omeprazole 20 mg OD before breakfast × 8 weeks; BD if severe) — drug of choice for erosive oesophagitis; maintenance at lowest effective dose / on-demand.","Adjuncts: H2 blockers (mild/nocturnal symptoms), antacids and alginates (raft formation — symptomatic), prokinetics (metoclopramide, domperidone, itopride) for regurgitation, baclofen (↓ TLESRs).","Refractory: surgery (laparoscopic Nissen fundoplication), endoscopic therapy; Barrett's oesophagus — long-term PPI + surveillance."],"GERD step-down: start PPI, then reduce to lowest effective dose."],
"Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease is a condition in which reflux of gastric contents into the oesophagus causes troublesome symptoms (heartburn, regurgitation, chest pain, chronic cough, hoarseness, asthma) and/or complications (erosive oesophagitis, stricture, Barrett oesophagus, adenocarcinoma). Pathogenesis: transient lower oesophageal sphincter relaxations (TLESRs), hypotensive LES, hiatus hernia, impaired oesophageal clearance, delayed gastric emptying, increased intra-abdominal pressure (obesity, pregnancy). Management: (1) lifestyle and dietary modifications — weight reduction, elevation of head end of bed by 15–20 cm, avoiding lying down for 3 hours after meals, small meals, avoiding trigger foods (fatty, spicy, chocolate, peppermint, citrus, coffee, carbonated drinks), stopping smoking and alcohol, avoiding tight clothing; reviewing drugs that lower LES pressure or injure mucosa (nitrates, CCBs, anticholinergics, TCAs, theophylline, benzodiazepines, progesterone, NSAIDs, bisphosphonates, doxycycline, KCl); (2) antacids (aluminium/magnesium hydroxide) and alginates (sodium alginate with bicarbonate forms a viscous raft on top of gastric contents) — quick symptomatic relief in mild, infrequent symptoms; (3) H2 receptor antagonists (famotidine 20–40 mg BD) — mild to moderate non-erosive disease, nocturnal breakthrough symptoms; tachyphylaxis limits long-term use; (4) proton pump inhibitors — most effective for symptom relief and healing of erosive oesophagitis (80–90% in 8 weeks); omeprazole 20 mg, pantoprazole 40 mg, esomeprazole 40 mg once daily 30–60 min before breakfast for 8 weeks; twice daily for partial responders; maintenance therapy at lowest effective dose or on-demand for non-erosive reflux disease; long-term for severe oesophagitis, Barrett oesophagus, strictures; vonoprazan (P-CAB) alternative; (5) prokinetic agents — metoclopramide, domperidone, itopride, mosapride — enhance LES tone and gastric emptying; adjunct for regurgitation, not recommended as monotherapy (EPS with metoclopramide, QT with domperidone); (6) baclofen (GABA-B agonist; reduces TLESRs) — limited use; (7) mucosal protectant sucralfate (pregnancy); (8) surgery — laparoscopic Nissen fundoplication for patients with refractory symptoms, large hiatus hernia, regurgitation, young patients unwilling for lifelong drugs; magnetic sphincter augmentation; endoscopic procedures (TIF, Stretta); bariatric surgery in obesity. Approach: step-up (antacids → H2 blockers → PPI) or step-down (start with PPI and reduce) — step-down preferred for rapid relief in moderate–severe disease. GERD in pregnancy: lifestyle, antacids/alginates, sucralfate, then H2 blockers or PPIs. Complications: strictures (dilatation + PPI), Barrett oesophagus (PPI + endoscopic surveillance; radiofrequency ablation for dysplasia).",
[["Stepwise treatment",[["Step","Measures"],["1","Lifestyle modification; antacids/alginates on demand"],["2","H2 blockers (mild/nocturnal)"],["3","PPI once daily × 8 weeks (drug of choice)"],["4","PPI twice daily ± prokinetic/baclofen"],["5","Surgery: Nissen fundoplication"]]],
["Drugs that worsen GERD",["Nitrates, CCBs","Anticholinergics, TCAs","Theophylline","Benzodiazepines, progesterone","NSAIDs, bisphosphonates, doxycycline (pill oesophagitis)"]]],
{c:"GERD management combines lifestyle measures with PPIs as the most effective therapy; adjuncts and surgery are reserved for partial responders and complicated disease."});

Z("pharm9",3,"ph",["Antiemetics prevent or treat nausea and vomiting by blocking receptors in the vomiting pathway; ondansetron (5-HT3 blocker) is used for chemotherapy and postoperative vomiting, and metoclopramide (D2 blocker and prokinetic) for gastric causes.","The vomiting centre receives messages from the gut (5-HT3), the CTZ (D2), the balance system (H1, M) and the brain; each antiemetic cuts a particular wire.",["Classification: 5-HT3 antagonists (ondansetron, granisetron, palonosetron), D2 antagonists (metoclopramide, domperidone, prochlorperazine, haloperidol), H1 antihistamines (promethazine, cyclizine, doxylamine, meclizine), anticholinergics (hyoscine), NK1 antagonists (aprepitant, fosaprepitant), corticosteroids (dexamethasone), cannabinoids (dronabinol), BZDs (lorazepam), olanzapine.","Metoclopramide: D2 blockade (CTZ, gut), 5-HT4 agonism, weak 5-HT3 block → antiemetic + prokinetic (↑ gastric emptying, LES tone); uses — GI-related vomiting, gastroparesis, GERD, migraine vomiting, before emergency anaesthesia; ADR — EPS (dystonia — children), drowsiness, hyperprolactinaemia, tardive dyskinesia (limit to 5 days).","Ondansetron: 5-HT3 antagonist in gut vagal afferents and CTZ; uses — CINV (with dexamethasone ± aprepitant), PONV, radiotherapy vomiting, hyperemesis (selected); ADR — headache, constipation, QT prolongation, serotonin syndrome (rare).","Motion sickness: hyoscine patch, promethazine, cyclizine; pregnancy: doxylamine–pyridoxine, promethazine, ondansetron."],"Motion sickness = antihistamines/hyoscine; chemotherapy = 5-HT3 + NK1 + dexamethasone."],
"Vomiting is coordinated by the vomiting centre (nucleus tractus solitarius and adjacent reticular formation) in the medulla, which receives inputs from (1) the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) in the area postrema (outside BBB; D2, 5-HT3, NK1, opioid receptors — drugs, toxins, uraemia, chemotherapy), (2) gastrointestinal vagal and splanchnic afferents (5-HT3 receptors stimulated by serotonin released from enterochromaffin cells after chemotherapy, irritation, distension), (3) vestibular apparatus (H1 and M1 receptors — motion sickness, labyrinthitis), (4) higher cortical centres (anticipatory vomiting, smells, sights, anxiety, raised ICP), (5) pharynx and heart. Classification of antiemetics: (1) anticholinergics — hyoscine (scopolamine), dicyclomine; (2) H1 antihistamines — promethazine, diphenhydramine, dimenhydrinate, doxylamine, cyclizine, meclizine, cinnarizine; (3) neuroleptics (D2 blockers) — chlorpromazine, prochlorperazine, haloperidol, droperidol, olanzapine; (4) prokinetic D2 blockers — metoclopramide, domperidone, itopride; (5) 5-HT3 antagonists — ondansetron, granisetron, dolasetron, tropisetron, palonosetron (long-acting, higher affinity); (6) NK1 receptor antagonists — aprepitant, fosaprepitant, rolapitant, netupitant; (7) adjuvants — dexamethasone, benzodiazepines (lorazepam), cannabinoids (dronabinol, nabilone). Metoclopramide: mechanism — antagonist at D2 receptors in CTZ (antiemetic) and in the gut (removes dopaminergic inhibition of cholinergic myenteric neurons); agonist at 5-HT4 receptors on enteric neurons (enhances ACh release); weak 5-HT3 antagonism at high doses; actions — increases LES tone, gastric antral contractions, relaxes pylorus and duodenum, accelerates gastric emptying and small intestinal transit (little effect on colon); central antiemetic; increases prolactin; pharmacokinetics — oral, IM, IV; crosses BBB; t½ 4–6 h. Uses: vomiting of various origins — postoperative, drug-induced, radiation, uraemia, migraine (improves absorption of analgesics), gastroenteritis (limited); diabetic and postoperative gastroparesis; GERD (adjunct); before emergency surgery and labour to promote gastric emptying (aspiration prophylaxis); facilitate small bowel intubation and radiological examination; persistent hiccups; not effective in motion sickness. Adverse effects: sedation, dizziness, restlessness, diarrhoea; extrapyramidal reactions — acute dystonia (more in children, young adults; treat with promethazine/benztropine), akathisia, parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia with long-term use (EMA/FDA restrict to ≤ 5 days and 0.5 mg/kg/day); hyperprolactinaemia (galactorrhoea, gynaecomastia, menstrual disturbances); neuroleptic malignant syndrome (rare); contraindicated in GI obstruction/perforation, pheochromocytoma, epilepsy, Parkinson disease. Domperidone does not cross BBB (fewer EPS) but prolongs QT. Ondansetron: mechanism — selective competitive antagonist of 5-HT3 receptors on vagal afferent terminals in the gut and in the CTZ/NTS; blocks serotonin-mediated emetic signals released by cytotoxic drugs and radiation; no D2 blockade (no EPS); pharmacokinetics — oral (including orally disintegrating tablets), IV, IM; bioavailability 60%; hepatic metabolism (CYP3A4, 2D6, 1A2); t½ 4–6 h; dose 4–8 mg (adult). Uses: chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV) — acute phase; highly emetogenic regimens (cisplatin) combined with dexamethasone and NK1 antagonist (aprepitant) and olanzapine; delayed CINV — NK1 antagonist and dexamethasone more effective; radiotherapy-induced vomiting; postoperative nausea and vomiting (prophylaxis and treatment); hyperemesis gravidarum (second-line; small risk of orofacial clefts in first trimester); acute gastroenteritis in children (single dose reduces need for IV fluids); drug-induced vomiting. Adverse effects: headache (most common), constipation, dizziness, fatigue, transient raised transaminases, QT prolongation (dose-dependent, avoid high IV doses), serotonin syndrome with other serotonergic drugs, hypersensitivity. Choice by cause: motion sickness — hyoscine transdermal, promethazine, cyclizine, cinnarizine; vertigo — betahistine, prochlorperazine; CINV — 5-HT3 + NK1 + dexamethasone ± olanzapine; anticipatory — lorazepam; PONV — ondansetron, dexamethasone, droperidol; pregnancy — doxylamine + pyridoxine (first-line), promethazine, metoclopramide, ondansetron; gastroparesis — metoclopramide, domperidone, erythromycin; opioid-induced — metoclopramide, haloperidol; palliative care — haloperidol, levomepromazine.",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs","Main use"],["5-HT3 antagonists","Ondansetron, granisetron, palonosetron","CINV, PONV"],["D2 antagonists (prokinetic)","Metoclopramide, domperidone","Gastric causes, gastroparesis"],["Neuroleptics","Prochlorperazine, haloperidol, droperidol","Drug-induced, PONV, vertigo"],["H1 antihistamines","Promethazine, cyclizine, doxylamine","Motion sickness, pregnancy"],["Anticholinergics","Hyoscine","Motion sickness"],["NK1 antagonists","Aprepitant, fosaprepitant","CINV (acute + delayed)"],["Adjuvants","Dexamethasone, lorazepam, dronabinol, olanzapine","CINV, anticipatory"]]],
["Metoclopramide vs ondansetron",[["Feature","Metoclopramide","Ondansetron"],["Receptor","D2 antagonist, 5-HT4 agonist","5-HT3 antagonist"],["Prokinetic","Yes","No (constipating)"],["Best use","GI causes, gastroparesis, GERD","CINV, PONV, radiotherapy"],["Motion sickness","Ineffective","Ineffective"],["EPS","Yes (dystonia, TD)","No"],["Prolactin ↑","Yes","No"],["Key ADR","EPS, sedation","Headache, constipation, QT"]]],
["Choice by cause",[["Cause","Drug"],["Motion sickness","Hyoscine, promethazine, cyclizine"],["Chemotherapy","Ondansetron + aprepitant + dexamethasone"],["Postoperative","Ondansetron, dexamethasone"],["Pregnancy","Doxylamine–pyridoxine, promethazine"],["Gastroparesis","Metoclopramide, domperidone"],["Vertigo","Prochlorperazine, betahistine"]]]],
{i:"Choosing an antiemetic requires identifying the cause and the receptor pathway involved.",f:"CTZ (D2, 5-HT3, NK1) ← drugs, toxins, chemo\n Gut vagal afferents (5-HT3) ← chemo, irritation\n Vestibular (H1, M1) ← motion\n Cortex ← anxiety, smell\n → Vomiting centre (NK1, 5-HT3, M, H1) → emesis",c:"Metoclopramide (antidopaminergic prokinetic) and ondansetron (5-HT3 antagonist) are key antiemetics with distinct indications; combination regimens are standard for chemotherapy-induced vomiting."});

Z("pharm9",4,"ph",["Diarrhoea is treated mainly by replacing lost water and salts with oral rehydration solution (ORS) and zinc; antibiotics are needed only for specific infections, and antimotility drugs are avoided in children.","ORS works because glucose drags sodium (and water) into intestinal cells through a co-transporter that stays working even in cholera.",["WHO low-osmolarity ORS (245 mOsm/L): NaCl 2.6 g, glucose anhydrous 13.5 g, KCl 1.5 g, trisodium citrate 2.9 g per litre; Na⁺ 75, glucose 75, K⁺ 20, Cl⁻ 65, citrate 10 mmol/L.","MOA: SGLT1 sodium–glucose cotransport intact in secretory diarrhoea; citrate corrects acidosis; K⁺ replaces loss.","Plan A (no dehydration): ORS after each stool + zinc 20 mg × 14 days (10 mg < 6 months) + continue feeding; Plan B (some): 75 mL/kg ORS over 4 h; Plan C (severe): IV Ringer lactate 100 mL/kg.","Drugs: antibiotics for cholera (doxycycline/azithromycin), dysentery (ciprofloxacin/azithromycin), amoebiasis/giardiasis (metronidazole); antimotility (loperamide) in adults only — not in bloody diarrhoea or children; racecadotril; probiotics."],"Diarrhoea = ORS + Zinc (+ feeding); antibiotics only if indicated."],
"Diarrhoea is the passage of three or more loose or watery stools in 24 hours; it remains a leading cause of child mortality (dehydration, malnutrition). Types: acute watery (including cholera), dysentery (blood in stool), persistent (≥ 14 days). Principles of treatment: (1) prevention and correction of dehydration and electrolyte imbalance (most important); (2) maintenance of nutrition; (3) zinc supplementation in children; (4) specific antimicrobial therapy only when indicated; (5) symptomatic drugs judiciously. Oral rehydration solution: based on the principle that Na⁺–glucose cotransport (SGLT1) in the brush border of small intestinal enterocytes remains intact even when cAMP-mediated secretion (cholera, ETEC) is active; glucose promotes Na⁺ absorption and water follows osmotically. WHO/UNICEF reduced osmolarity ORS (since 2002; total osmolarity 245 mOsm/L) — composition per litre: sodium chloride 2.6 g, glucose anhydrous 13.5 g, potassium chloride 1.5 g, trisodium citrate dihydrate 2.9 g; giving Na⁺ 75 mmol/L, Cl⁻ 65, glucose 75, K⁺ 20, citrate 10; advantages over old ORS (311 mOsm/L, Na⁺ 90) — reduced stool output, less vomiting, fewer IV infusions, lower risk of hypernatraemia. Home-available fluids: rice water, salted lassi, coconut water, sugar–salt solution. Rice-based ORS and ReSoMal (for severe acute malnutrition — lower Na⁺, higher K⁺) are variants. Management by degree of dehydration (WHO/IMNCI): Plan A (no dehydration) — home treatment: extra fluids/ORS after each loose stool (< 2 years 50–100 mL; 2–10 years 100–200 mL; older as much as wanted), continue breastfeeding and feeding, zinc, return if danger signs; Plan B (some dehydration) — ORS 75 mL/kg over 4 hours in health facility, reassess; Plan C (severe dehydration) — IV Ringer lactate (or normal saline) 100 mL/kg: infants < 12 months 30 mL/kg in 1 h then 70 mL/kg in 5 h; older 30 mL/kg in 30 min then 70 mL/kg in 2.5 h; ORS 5 mL/kg/h as soon as able to drink; nasogastric ORS if IV not possible. Zinc: 20 mg/day for 10–14 days (10 mg in infants < 6 months) — reduces duration and severity of episode and incidence for next 2–3 months (improves absorption, immunity, mucosal repair). Antimicrobials (indicated only for): cholera (doxycycline single dose 300 mg, azithromycin in children), bacillary dysentery (ciprofloxacin, azithromycin, ceftriaxone), amoebic dysentery and giardiasis (metronidazole, tinidazole), Clostridioides difficile (oral vancomycin, fidaxomicin), traveller's diarrhoea (azithromycin, rifaximin), typhoid; most acute watery diarrhoea is viral (rotavirus) — antibiotics not indicated. Antidiarrhoeal drugs: antimotility — loperamide (peripheral μ-opioid agonist; does not cross BBB; adults with non-bloody diarrhoea; contraindicated in children < 4–12 years, dysentery, C. difficile, ulcerative colitis — risk of toxic megacolon, ileus), diphenoxylate with atropine, codeine; antisecretory — racecadotril (enkephalinase inhibitor), bismuth subsalicylate, octreotide (carcinoid, VIPoma, chemotherapy diarrhoea); adsorbents (kaolin, pectin, activated charcoal — little value); probiotics (Lactobacillus GG, Saccharomyces boulardii — modest reduction in duration); bile acid sequestrants (bile acid diarrhoea); clonidine (diabetic diarrhoea). Prevention: exclusive breastfeeding, safe water, sanitation, handwashing, rotavirus and measles vaccination, vitamin A supplementation.",
[["ORS composition (WHO low osmolarity)",[["Ingredient","g/L","Ion","mmol/L"],["Sodium chloride","2.6","Na⁺","75"],["Glucose anhydrous","13.5","Glucose","75"],["Potassium chloride","1.5","K⁺","20"],["Trisodium citrate","2.9","Cl⁻ / citrate","65 / 10"],["Total osmolarity","—","—","245 mOsm/L"]]],
["WHO treatment plans",[["Plan","Dehydration","Treatment"],["A","None","Home ORS after each stool, zinc, feeding"],["B","Some","ORS 75 mL/kg over 4 h"],["C","Severe","IV Ringer lactate 100 mL/kg"]]],
["Antibiotic indications",["Cholera: doxycycline/azithromycin","Bacillary dysentery: ciprofloxacin/azithromycin","Amoebiasis, giardiasis: metronidazole","C. difficile: vancomycin oral","Not for viral watery diarrhoea"]]],
{c:"ORS with zinc and continued feeding is the cornerstone of diarrhoea management; antibiotics and antimotility drugs have limited, specific indications."});

Z("pharm9",5,"ph",["Racecadotril is an antisecretory antidiarrhoeal drug that blocks enkephalinase, increasing natural enkephalins in the gut which reduce intestinal secretion of water and electrolytes without slowing gut movement.","It turns down the 'tap' of fluid secretion in the gut while leaving the 'drain' (motility) open — unlike loperamide.",["MOA: prodrug → thiorphan, inhibits enkephalinase (neutral endopeptidase) in intestinal epithelium → ↑ enkephalins → δ-opioid receptor → ↓ cAMP → ↓ hypersecretion.","No effect on motility/transit; does not cross BBB; no constipation or abuse.","Uses: acute watery diarrhoea in children (> 3 months) and adults as adjunct to ORS; reduces stool output and duration.","ADR: headache, rash, rarely angioedema (caution with ACE inhibitors); dose 1.5 mg/kg TDS (children), 100 mg TDS adults; max 7 days."],"Racecadotril = antisecretory, not antimotility."],
"Racecadotril (acetorphan) is an oral antisecretory antidiarrhoeal agent. Mechanism: it is a prodrug rapidly hydrolysed to its active metabolite thiorphan, a selective inhibitor of enkephalinase (neutral endopeptidase, NEP 24.11) located in the intestinal epithelium; inhibition protects endogenous enkephalins released by enteric neurons from degradation; enkephalins act on δ-opioid receptors on enterocytes, reducing cAMP and thereby decreasing hypersecretion of water and electrolytes into the intestinal lumen (secretory diarrhoea, e.g., cholera toxin, rotavirus) — without affecting basal secretion or intestinal motility. Unlike loperamide, it does not cause intestinal hypomotility, rebound constipation or bacterial overgrowth, does not cross the blood–brain barrier (no central opioid effects or abuse potential) and is safe in children. Pharmacokinetics: rapid oral absorption, onset within 30 minutes, effect lasts about 8 hours; metabolised to inactive metabolites; renal excretion. Dose: children 1.5 mg/kg three times daily (granules), adults 100 mg three times daily, until two normal stools (maximum 7 days). Uses: symptomatic treatment of acute diarrhoea in infants (> 3 months), children and adults, always as an adjunct to oral rehydration solution and zinc, not a replacement; reduces stool output, duration of diarrhoea and ORS requirement in several trials (evidence moderate; not included in WHO diarrhoea guidelines). Adverse effects: generally well tolerated — headache, drowsiness, rash, urticaria, erythema multiforme, angioedema (rare; risk increased with ACE inhibitors, which also inhibit bradykinin degradation), hypokalaemia (rare). Contraindications: hypersensitivity, bloody diarrhoea/dysentery or high fever (invasive infections require antimicrobials), antibiotic-associated diarrhoea, chronic diarrhoea, renal/hepatic impairment (caution), lactose/sucrose intolerance (formulation).",
[["Racecadotril vs loperamide",[["Feature","Racecadotril","Loperamide"],["Mechanism","Enkephalinase inhibitor (antisecretory)","μ-opioid agonist (antimotility)"],["Motility","Unchanged","↓"],["Rebound constipation","No","Yes"],["Children","Safe (> 3 months)","Avoid"],["Dysentery","Avoid","Contraindicated"]]]],
{c:"Racecadotril reduces intestinal hypersecretion by preserving enkephalins without slowing gut motility, making it a useful adjunct to ORS in acute watery diarrhoea, including children."});

Z("pharm9",6,"ph",["Laxatives and purgatives promote bowel evacuation; they are classified as bulk-forming, stool softeners, osmotic and stimulant agents, and are used for constipation and bowel preparation.","They work by adding bulk (fibre sponge), softening stool (detergent), drawing water in (osmotic) or poking the gut to move (stimulant).",["Bulk-forming: dietary fibre, ispaghula (psyllium), methylcellulose — first-line, take with water; 1–3 days.","Stool softeners/lubricants: docusate, liquid paraffin (lipid pneumonia, ↓ fat-soluble vitamins).","Osmotic: lactulose (also hepatic encephalopathy), polyethylene glycol (PEG — bowel prep), magnesium sulfate/hydroxide, sodium phosphate (nephropathy), glycerine suppository.","Stimulant: bisacodyl, senna, sodium picosulfate, castor oil; newer — lubiprostone, linaclotide, prucalopride; opioid-induced — methylnaltrexone, naloxegol. CI: intestinal obstruction, undiagnosed abdominal pain; abuse → cathartic colon, hypokalaemia."],"Lactulose 'double duty': constipation + hepatic encephalopathy."],
"Laxatives promote evacuation of soft formed stools; purgatives (cathartics) produce more fluid evacuation; the difference is often dose-dependent. Classification: (1) bulk-forming — dietary fibre (bran), ispaghula (psyllium husk), methylcellulose, sterculia; hydrophilic, absorb water, increase stool volume and soften it, stimulating peristalsis; act in 1–3 days; must be taken with plenty of water (risk of obstruction); safest for long-term use, IBS, haemorrhoids, pregnancy; flatulence, bloating; (2) stool softeners (emollients) — docusate sodium (anionic detergent; lowers surface tension, allows water penetration; mild); (3) lubricants — liquid paraffin (coats stool; leaks per anum, lipid pneumonia on aspiration, malabsorption of fat-soluble vitamins — avoided), arachis oil enema; (4) osmotic laxatives — non-absorbable sugars: lactulose (fermented by colonic bacteria to lactic and acetic acids — osmotic effect and lowered pH; acts in 1–3 days; flatulence, cramps; also used in hepatic encephalopathy — traps NH3 as NH4⁺), lactitol, sorbitol; polyethylene glycol (macrogol) with electrolytes (constipation, faecal impaction, bowel preparation before colonoscopy — large volume; well tolerated); saline purgatives — magnesium sulphate (Epsom salt), magnesium hydroxide (milk of magnesia), sodium sulphate, sodium phosphate (bowel prep; acute phosphate nephropathy), act in 1–3 h; avoid magnesium in renal failure; glycerine suppository (rectal irritation and osmotic action; quick); (5) stimulant (irritant) purgatives — diphenylmethanes: bisacodyl (oral 6–10 h, suppository 15–60 min), sodium picosulfate; anthraquinones: senna, cascara, aloes (activated by colonic bacteria; overnight action; melanosis coli with chronic use); castor oil (ricinoleic acid; small intestine; 2–6 h; may induce labour — avoid in pregnancy); stimulate enteric neurons and increase secretion; cramps; chronic abuse — cathartic colon (atonic), hypokalaemia, dehydration, protein-losing enteropathy; (6) newer agents for chronic constipation — prucalopride (selective 5-HT4 agonist, prokinetic), lubiprostone (ClC-2 chloride channel activator), linaclotide and plecanatide (guanylate cyclase-C agonists; IBS-C), elobixibat (ileal bile acid transporter inhibitor); (7) peripherally acting μ-opioid antagonists for opioid-induced constipation — methylnaltrexone (SC), naloxegol, naldemedine, alvimopan (postoperative ileus). Uses: functional constipation (after diet and lifestyle — fibre, fluids, exercise, toilet habit), constipation in pregnancy (bulk, lactulose), elderly, bedridden, opioid-induced (stimulant + softener prophylactically, PAMORAs), to avoid straining (haemorrhoids, anal fissure, hernia, post-MI, post-surgery), bowel preparation before colonoscopy, radiology or surgery (PEG, sodium picosulfate), faecal impaction (enemas, PEG), hepatic encephalopathy (lactulose), poisoning (to hasten elimination — rarely), with anthelmintics (historically). Contraindications: intestinal obstruction, undiagnosed acute abdominal pain (appendicitis — perforation risk), inflammatory bowel disease (acute), severe dehydration; stimulant purgatives in pregnancy. Laxative abuse leads to electrolyte disturbances (hypokalaemia, metabolic alkalosis or acidosis), dehydration, cathartic colon, dependence.",
[["Classification",[["Type","Drugs","Onset","Note"],["Bulk-forming","Ispaghula, methylcellulose, bran","1–3 days","Safest; take water"],["Softener/lubricant","Docusate, liquid paraffin","1–3 days","Paraffin: lipid pneumonia"],["Osmotic sugar","Lactulose, lactitol","1–3 days","Hepatic encephalopathy"],["PEG","Macrogol","1–3 days / hours (prep)","Bowel preparation"],["Saline","MgSO4, Mg(OH)2, Na phosphate","1–3 h","Avoid Mg in renal failure"],["Stimulant","Bisacodyl, senna, picosulfate, castor oil","6–12 h","Cramps; abuse → cathartic colon"],["Newer","Prucalopride, lubiprostone, linaclotide","Variable","Chronic constipation"],["PAMORA","Methylnaltrexone, naloxegol","Hours","Opioid-induced constipation"]]],
["Contraindications",["Intestinal obstruction","Undiagnosed abdominal pain","Acute IBD","Severe dehydration"]]],
{c:"Laxatives are chosen according to need: bulk-formers first-line, osmotic agents for chronic use and bowel preparation, stimulants for short-term use, and newer agents for refractory or opioid-induced constipation."});

Z("pharm9",7,"ph",["Inflammatory bowel disease (ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease) is treated with 5-aminosalicylates, corticosteroids, immunosuppressants (azathioprine, methotrexate) and biologics (anti-TNF like infliximab, vedolizumab, ustekinumab), chosen by severity.","IBD is an overactive immune fire in the gut: 5-ASA cools mild fire locally, steroids douse flares, immunomodulators and biologics keep it from rekindling.",["5-ASA (aminosalicylates): sulfasalazine, mesalamine (mesalazine), olsalazine, balsalazide — first-line for mild–moderate UC (induction + maintenance); topical forms for proctitis; sulfasalazine ADR — rash, oligospermia, folate deficiency, agranulocytosis.","Corticosteroids: prednisolone (oral), hydrocortisone IV (severe), budesonide (ileal Crohn's, microscopic colitis; high first pass) — induction only, not maintenance.","Immunomodulators: azathioprine/6-MP (check TPMT), methotrexate (Crohn's), cyclosporine/tacrolimus (severe UC rescue).","Biologics/small molecules: anti-TNF (infliximab, adalimumab — screen TB, HBV), vedolizumab (gut-selective α4β7), ustekinumab (IL-12/23), JAK inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib), ozanimod (S1P)."],"IBD ladder: 5-ASA → steroids → AZA/MTX → biologics → surgery."],
"Inflammatory bowel disease comprises ulcerative colitis (UC; continuous mucosal inflammation from rectum) and Crohn disease (CD; transmural, skip lesions anywhere in GI tract), resulting from dysregulated immune responses to gut flora in genetically susceptible individuals. Goals: induce and maintain remission (clinical and mucosal healing), prevent complications, reduce steroid use, improve quality of life. Drugs: (1) aminosalicylates (5-ASA) — act topically on colonic mucosa: inhibit COX and lipoxygenase (reduced PGs and LTs), scavenge free radicals, inhibit NF-κB and cytokines, activate PPAR-γ; preparations designed to deliver 5-ASA to distal gut: sulfasalazine (5-ASA linked by azo bond to sulfapyridine; cleaved by colonic bacterial azoreductase; sulfapyridine causes most adverse effects — nausea, headache, rash, haemolysis in G6PD deficiency, agranulocytosis, hepatitis, reversible oligospermia, folate malabsorption — give folic acid; also used in rheumatoid arthritis), mesalamine/mesalazine (pH-dependent delayed release or time-release; enemas, suppositories, foam), olsalazine (5-ASA dimer; secretory diarrhoea), balsalazide (prodrug); uses — first-line for induction and maintenance of mild–moderate UC (oral + rectal combination most effective; suppositories for proctitis), limited role in CD; adverse effects of mesalamine — nephritis (monitor renal function), pancreatitis, pericarditis, hypersensitivity, paradoxical worsening; (2) corticosteroids — prednisolone 40 mg/day orally tapered over 8 weeks for moderate disease; IV hydrocortisone 100 mg 6-hourly or methylprednisolone for acute severe UC; budesonide (high first-pass hepatic metabolism, fewer systemic effects) — controlled ileal release for ileocaecal Crohn's, MMX formulation for UC; rectal steroids; used for induction only — ineffective and harmful for maintenance; steroid dependence/resistance requires escalation; (3) immunomodulators (thiopurines) — azathioprine (2–2.5 mg/kg/day) and 6-mercaptopurine (1–1.5 mg/kg); slow onset (3–6 months); maintenance of steroid-induced remission, steroid sparing, with anti-TNF to reduce antibody formation; check TPMT/NUDT15 activity before starting (myelotoxicity risk); adverse effects — bone marrow suppression, hepatotoxicity, pancreatitis, infections, lymphoma and non-melanoma skin cancer; methotrexate (25 mg weekly IM/SC, then 15 mg) — Crohn's induction/maintenance; folic acid supplementation; hepatotoxicity, teratogenic; calcineurin inhibitors — cyclosporine IV (2 mg/kg/day) and tacrolimus as rescue in steroid-refractory acute severe UC (bridge to thiopurines); (4) biologics — anti-TNF-α monoclonal antibodies: infliximab (chimeric IV; induction 5 mg/kg at 0, 2, 6 weeks then 8-weekly), adalimumab and golimumab (SC, human); certolizumab pegol (CD); indications — moderate to severe UC/CD refractory to conventional therapy, steroid-dependent disease, fistulising Crohn's, acute severe UC rescue; risks — infusion reactions, serious infections (reactivation of latent TB — screen with IGRA/chest X-ray; hepatitis B — screen), opportunistic infections, demyelination, heart failure worsening, lupus-like syndrome, psoriasiform lesions, lymphoma (hepatosplenic T-cell lymphoma in young males with thiopurine combination), antidrug antibodies (loss of response); anti-integrin: vedolizumab (α4β7; gut-selective; low systemic infection risk; no PML), natalizumab (α4; PML risk; restricted); anti-IL-12/23: ustekinumab; anti-IL-23: risankizumab, mirikizumab, guselkumab; (5) small molecules — JAK inhibitors: tofacitinib, upadacitinib, filgotinib (oral; UC/CD; herpes zoster, thrombosis, MACE, lipid rise); S1P receptor modulators: ozanimod, etrasimod (UC; bradycardia, macular oedema); (6) antibiotics — metronidazole, ciprofloxacin for perianal Crohn's, pouchitis, abscesses; rifaximin; (7) supportive — iron, vitamin B12, folate, calcium/vitamin D, nutritional therapy (exclusive enteral nutrition in paediatric CD), VTE prophylaxis in hospitalised patients, avoid NSAIDs, opioids and antidiarrhoeals in acute severe colitis (toxic megacolon); (8) surgery — colectomy with ileal pouch–anal anastomosis (curative in UC), resection/strictureplasty in CD for complications. Treatment strategy: mild–moderate UC — 5-ASA oral + topical; moderate — add oral steroids; maintenance with 5-ASA, thiopurines; moderate–severe or refractory — biologics or small molecules; acute severe UC — IV steroids, rescue infliximab or cyclosporine on day 3–5 if no response, colectomy; Crohn's — budesonide (mild ileal), steroids, early biologics ± immunomodulator ('top-down' in high-risk), methotrexate; smoking cessation in CD.",
[["Drug classes",[["Class","Drugs","Role"],["5-ASA","Sulfasalazine, mesalamine, balsalazide","Mild–moderate UC: induction + maintenance"],["Corticosteroids","Prednisolone, IV hydrocortisone, budesonide","Induction only"],["Thiopurines","Azathioprine, 6-MP","Maintenance, steroid-sparing"],["Methotrexate","—","Crohn's"],["Calcineurin inhibitors","Cyclosporine, tacrolimus","Rescue in acute severe UC"],["Anti-TNF","Infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab","Moderate–severe, fistulising"],["Anti-integrin","Vedolizumab","Gut-selective biologic"],["Anti-IL-12/23","Ustekinumab, risankizumab","Moderate–severe"],["JAK / S1P","Tofacitinib, upadacitinib; ozanimod","Oral advanced therapy"],["Antibiotics","Metronidazole, ciprofloxacin","Perianal CD, pouchitis"]]],
["Precautions",["TPMT testing before azathioprine","TB and HBV screening before biologics","Folic acid with sulfasalazine/methotrexate","Avoid NSAIDs, antimotility agents in severe colitis","Steroids not for maintenance"]]],
{i:"Therapy of IBD is tailored to disease type, extent and severity, increasingly with early use of biologics in high-risk patients.",f:"Mild UC → 5-ASA (oral + rectal)\n Moderate → + prednisolone → maintenance 5-ASA/thiopurine\n Severe/refractory → anti-TNF, vedolizumab, ustekinumab, JAK inhibitors\n Acute severe UC → IV steroids → infliximab/cyclosporine → colectomy",c:"IBD drugs range from topical 5-ASA through corticosteroids and immunomodulators to targeted biologics and small molecules; induction and maintenance strategies are chosen by disease severity with careful safety monitoring."});

Z("pharm9",8,"ph",["Prokinetics enhance coordinated gut movement and gastric emptying; they include dopamine D2 antagonists (metoclopramide, domperidone, itopride), 5-HT4 agonists (prucalopride, mosapride) and motilin agonists (erythromycin).","Prokinetics press the gut's 'accelerator' — mainly by increasing acetylcholine release from enteric nerves — so food moves forward instead of sitting or refluxing.",["Metoclopramide: D2 block + 5-HT4 agonism; central + peripheral; EPS, hyperprolactinaemia.","Domperidone: peripheral D2 blocker (doesn't cross BBB) — fewer EPS; QT prolongation/sudden death at high doses; galactorrhoea (pituitary outside BBB).","Itopride: D2 antagonist + AChE inhibitor; few CNS/cardiac effects; mosapride, prucalopride (selective 5-HT4 — chronic constipation); erythromycin (motilin agonist — gastroparesis, before endoscopy for GI bleed); neostigmine (acute colonic pseudo-obstruction).","Uses: gastroparesis (diabetic, postoperative), GERD (adjunct), functional dyspepsia, nausea/vomiting, emptying before emergency anaesthesia, small-bowel intubation; CI — GI obstruction, perforation, bleeding."],"Erythromycin = 'motilin mimic' for gastroparesis."],
"Prokinetic drugs selectively enhance coordinated gastrointestinal motility and transit of contents, mainly by increasing acetylcholine release at enteric neuromuscular junctions without disrupting physiological rhythm. Classification: (1) dopamine D2 receptor antagonists — metoclopramide (also 5-HT4 agonist, 5-HT3 antagonist), domperidone, levosulpiride, itopride (also acetylcholinesterase inhibitor); (2) 5-HT4 receptor agonists — cisapride (withdrawn — QT, torsades), mosapride, prucalopride (highly selective; chronic constipation), tegaserod (restricted — cardiovascular); renzapride, velusetrag; (3) motilin receptor agonists — erythromycin, azithromycin (macrolides at subantimicrobial doses mimic motilin, inducing migrating motor complex phase III — strong antral contractions); (4) cholinomimetics — bethanechol (muscarinic), neostigmine (AChE inhibitor; acute colonic pseudo-obstruction — Ogilvie syndrome); (5) ghrelin agonists (relamorelin — investigational). Dopamine normally inhibits ACh release from myenteric neurons via D2 receptors; D2 antagonists remove this inhibition. Metoclopramide: increases LES tone, antral contractions and gastric emptying, relaxes pylorus, increases small bowel transit; central antiemetic (CTZ D2 block); crosses BBB — extrapyramidal reactions (acute dystonia, akathisia, parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia — limit to 5 days), drowsiness, hyperprolactinaemia, NMS. Domperidone: peripheral D2 antagonist that does not cross BBB readily — fewer EPS; acts on CTZ (outside BBB) — antiemetic; increases prolactin (pituitary outside BBB — galactorrhoea, gynaecomastia; used to increase lactation off-label); QT prolongation, ventricular arrhythmias and sudden cardiac death at doses > 30 mg/day, elderly, with CYP3A4 inhibitors — restricted to 10 mg up to TDS for ≤ 7 days; uses — nausea and vomiting, gastroparesis, Parkinson patients on levodopa (counteracts peripheral dopaminergic nausea without worsening parkinsonism), functional dyspepsia. Itopride: D2 antagonist plus AChE inhibitor; does not cross BBB, minimal CYP metabolism and no QT effect; functional dyspepsia, GERD. Mosapride: selective 5-HT4 agonist; functional dyspepsia, GERD. Prucalopride: highly selective 5-HT4 agonist acting on colon; chronic idiopathic constipation unresponsive to laxatives; headache, diarrhoea. Erythromycin: IV 3 mg/kg (or 250 mg) 30–60 min before upper GI endoscopy in acute bleeding (clears stomach of blood), diabetic gastroparesis (tachyphylaxis with prolonged use), feeding intolerance in ICU; QT prolongation, CYP3A4 interactions, antimicrobial resistance. Uses of prokinetics: gastroparesis (diabetic, idiopathic, postoperative, drug-induced), GERD (adjunct, regurgitation), functional (dysmotility-like) dyspepsia, nausea and vomiting (antiemetic action), postoperative ileus, emptying of stomach before emergency surgery or labour (aspiration prevention), facilitating small-bowel intubation and radiological studies, chronic constipation (prucalopride), Ogilvie syndrome (neostigmine), intractable hiccups. Contraindications: mechanical GI obstruction, perforation, haemorrhage; pheochromocytoma (metoclopramide — hypertensive crisis); prolactinoma; epilepsy (metoclopramide); cardiac conduction disorders (domperidone, erythromycin).",
[["Classification",[["Class","Drugs","Notes"],["D2 antagonists","Metoclopramide, domperidone, levosulpiride","Antiemetic + prokinetic"],["D2 antagonist + AChE inhibitor","Itopride","No QT, no EPS"],["5-HT4 agonists","Mosapride, prucalopride (cisapride withdrawn)","Prucalopride: constipation"],["Motilin agonists","Erythromycin, azithromycin","Gastroparesis, pre-endoscopy"],["Cholinomimetics","Bethanechol, neostigmine","Ogilvie syndrome"]]],
["Metoclopramide vs domperidone",[["Feature","Metoclopramide","Domperidone"],["Crosses BBB","Yes","No"],["EPS","Yes","Rare"],["Prolactin ↑","Yes","Yes"],["QT prolongation","Minimal","Yes"],["Parkinson patients","Avoid","Preferred"]]]],
{c:"Prokinetics improve gastrointestinal transit through dopamine antagonism, 5-HT4 agonism or motilin agonism; their choice balances efficacy with EPS and cardiac safety."});
